WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.070
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I just wanted to start off
by really thanking Simon and the team, including

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.090
Simon Enright, who is here as well, I think,
in the audience, for the way that the Long-Term

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.910
Plan was produced and delivered. I think it
– you know, it’s a fantastic – I don’t

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:25.690
think there’s any other health system in
the world that could produce a report like

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:31.800
this, actually, speaking for the whole of
the health system. So, I think it was – congratulations,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.470
Simon, to you, and actually all the team,
and all the many hours that went into producing

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.890
it.
Interestingly, in my report, I’d just say,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:48.060
I had just two visits I just would mention.
One was to South Yorkshire, one to Manchester.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.900
And very interesting talking particularly
in primary care, just the extent to which

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.440
the Long-Term Plan is actually – you know,
this is not just a plan, this is actually

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.100
working, you know, coming out of the Five
Year Forward View five – well, nearly five

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.970
years ago. Actually, on the ground, there
is a lot happening, and a lot of enthusiasm

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:13.430
for it. I mean, interesting, Nikki’s in
the audience, as well, today, but going to

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:19.900
Steve Kell’s practice in South Yorkshire,
you know, seeing they’ve got three paramedics,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.330
they’ve got pharmacists, they’ve got arranged
with the local care homes in a way that looked

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.660
to be like the future of primary care, which
we’re going to talk about in detail a bit

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:35.450
later with Ian, and Richard from the GPs – from
the BMA part of the GPs is going to be here

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.450
as well talking about that. But also, it’s
happening in Manchester; I was very struck

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:45.750
in Manchester how the social determiners of
care are really a driving force there, that,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.820
you know, whereas it is still very important
about the – sort of the more acute-based

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.120
targets that we have, but a lot of the work
they’re doing on the ground around prevention

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.150
is starting to bear fruit. So, in both those
visits, it was really reinforcing everything

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.940
that was in the Long-Term Plan.
And then the other thing that I’d just mention

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:12.020
is that in December, we – or I’d say the
GeL completed the sequencing of 100,000 whole

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:20.340
genomes. And in the same month, NHS England
commissioned the first CAR T cell therapies

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:25.840
at Great Ormond Street for five children.
And that really has got the attention of the

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:32.660
world. I went to the JP Morgan conference
for the Office of Life Sciences, and actually,

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.610
far from us having to try and get that attention
of the pharmaceutical world, they are beating

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.370
a path to our door for the work that we’re
doing in genomics, but also seeing that the

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:46.630
NHS is at the forefront of commissioning and
coming up with ideas of how we price some

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:52.440
of these new therapies. So, I thought that
was really interesting. And going to Manchester,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.069
not only did I see some really interesting
work being on the ground in primary care,

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:03.670
but also, the genomics facility at the Manchester
Royal Infirmary is doing some fantastic world,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.690
so there’s that that – sort of in the
same integrated care system in Manchester,

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.110
you see on-the-ground work going on as well
as world-class sort of genomic medicine at

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:16.370
the same time. So, that was really interesting.
So, that was really all that I was going to

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.989
say by way of my report, Simon, so over to
you.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:26.590
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you. I’ll just add
very briefly to that, particularly thanking

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:32.950
all of our teams who are at the moment working
on the oversight of the pressures that are

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.849
in the NHS for winter, and we’ll hear from
Pauline and Matthew on that. There’s a big

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:44.770
job of work to finish the ‘18/19 financial
year and prepare for next year as the first

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:53.450
year of implementation of the Long-Term Plan.
We may want to touch on the work underway

00:03:53.450 --> 00:04:00.220
on Brexit contingency planning. An enormous
effort going on, several hundred people assigned

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:08.099
to that task. But specifically, today, we
are going to be addressing the first pillar

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.950
of implementation of the Long-Term Plan, as
you say, David, through the new GP contract

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:18.069
and the primary care networks, and I would
echo your thanks to our team and also to Richard

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:24.749
Vautrey and the GPC for the constructive way
in which we’ve been able to work together

00:04:24.749 --> 00:04:28.800
on that.
We – just two other things to say. We have

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:35.270
been invited by the Health Committee and by
government to put together more detailed proposals

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:42.599
on the legislation that we prefigured in the
Long-Term Plan, and so those proposals will

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:49.819
be issued for engagement across the Health
Service and more widely in the month of February,

00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:57.520
which I guess begins tomorrow. Is it tomorrow?
And similarly, we will be advertising for

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.120
co-chairs of the NHS Assembly within the next
couple of weeks. So, we’re going to have

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.379
obviously a detailed conversation around a
lot of those things in the months to come,

00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:10.759
but just – those will have been kicked off
between now and our next public Board meeting.

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:17.060
Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent. Anyone want to comment?

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.589
Noel and Joanne, you both went on a visit
up to Yorkshire, Leeds, I think, didn’t

00:05:21.589 --> 00:05:24.780
you, to look at integrated care there? Do
you have any reflections on that?

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:32.210
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: We did. We went to both Leeds,
to understand how a lot of technology we’ve

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:37.539
delivered over the last five years has been
delivered into acute settings and outpatients.

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:44.340
And then we went to Wakefield by design, to
understand how ICSs are coming together in

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:53.589
some of our less privileged part of the country.
It was quite an extraordinary visit. We didn’t

00:05:53.589 --> 00:06:02.289
anticipate seeing quite as much in the way
of innovation and delivery and practical solutions

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:07.189
as we saw. I was particularly – I don’t
know what Joanne thinks – I was particularly

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:12.479
impressed with the way the ICSs are coming
together, and the cohesion around the management

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:17.990
team was, I thought, quite extraordinary.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Well, I – as well as echoing

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.479
that, I’d just – I would like to mention
Dorset, because there was another visit to

00:06:21.479 --> 00:06:26.169
Dorset in the last couple of weeks, and – where
we saw in a very different part of the country

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:31.559
a vision of integrated care coming together.
And I think one of the standouts of that particular

00:06:31.559 --> 00:06:39.990
visit was sitting in a room with community
nurses, representatives of the – acute care

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:47.969
and the local authority, with the GPs dialling
in, to talk through individual patients who

00:06:47.969 --> 00:06:53.870
are vulnerable and who are – need care from
a number of different sources, and those people,

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:59.520
you know, getting that from an integrated,
collective view with all the right people

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.819
in the room and people who knew them and were
able to provide exactly the care that they

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:08.569
needed in the most effective way. So, that
was an extremely powerful experience.

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:13.129
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: I think also – I mean,
in Dorset, the strength of the relationships

00:07:13.129 --> 00:07:18.490
and the trust that’s being built locally
was demonstrable to everybody, and the care

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:23.759
in which people were putting to really finding
solutions to what often are very vulnerable.

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:31.580
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think it will be covered
more when we get onto the new GP contract,

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:38.699
how the primary care is absolutely – so,
we will come to that.

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:40.179
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I think the other thing, David,
from that was just, obviously, you’ve got

00:07:40.179 --> 00:07:43.939
the Bournemouth and Poole reconfiguration,
which I think’s been going for ten years.

00:07:43.939 --> 00:07:49.490
I think one of the things we have to do is
think about this, how can we help local systems,

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:54.650
where they’re making the right clinical
decisions and the right decisions for their

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.039
populations, find a way to do some of those
things a bit quicker.

00:07:58.039 --> 00:08:04.129
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Yeah. So, I – just if I could
just add to that, while I was in Dorset, I

00:08:04.129 --> 00:08:09.580
had an opportunity to have an impromptu focus
group at the railway station with five very

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:18.889
voluble members of the Poole Town’s Women’s
Guild, who were I think very kindly talking

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:24.949
to me about their views of their local health
system. And it was clear that the – that

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:30.740
what was happening in relation to the hospitals
was really sort of top of mind, and being

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.740
able to make those happen is going to be very
important.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And I see that Bren is in the
audience, I see, back there, and I’m going

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:43.280
to be spending a Gloucestershire with Bren,
seeing how the voluntary sector on the ground

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:47.130
can really contribute to this as well, and
how that is tied into the primary care system,

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:52.180
as well, is really important.
Excellent. So, shall we move on to the CCG

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:57.220
allocations, Matt?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Thank you, Chair. So, we’ve

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.970
obviously discussed the disposition of resources
for ‘19/20, and the following four years,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:09.360
in some detail in Board committees previously.
But we will give final – will be recommending

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:16.410
final sign-off of those proposals by the Board
today. The paper that we have before us starts

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:23.760
by confirming the final settlement agreed
with the government for the five-year period.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.660
As you’ll recall, there were changes in
the GDP deflators, which required some adjustments

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:37.450
to be made to the settlement, but we now have
the final numbers before us, as a consequence

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:43.470
of which, the real-terms growth rate over
the five years remains at 3.4%, with a profile

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:53.780
of 3.6%, 3.1%, 3%, 3%, 4.1%, as set out in
the table on page three of the paper, or page

00:09:53.780 --> 00:10:02.840
11 of the Board pack. The paper then goes
on to – it starts by setting out how we

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:09.820
will distribute those resources to the different
aspects of our commissioning operations, and

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:14.280
the starting point for that is to ensure that
we fund a realistic and sustainable level

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:20.390
of activity for all of our commissioning streams,
whether that’s via CCGs or through specialised

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:24.190
commissioning, or our other direct commissioning
functions. We principally do that by looking

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:29.900
at the levels of growth over the recent past
and making adjustments for what we know about

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.510
population growth and other factors going
forward.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:40.070
We then take into account the price pressures
that we know the system will face over the

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:46.010
coming period. And in particular, there are
some significant movements over this period,

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:55.070
so those significant pay awards in 2018/19
are reflected in the allocations for 2019/20.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:11:00.470
And some movements in the Provider Sustainability
Fund, which I’ll come on to a little later.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:05.000
Crucially, as well, we have protected funding
for the implementation of existing Five Year

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.910
Forward View commitments, particularly in
respect of mental health, primary care and

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.960
cancer services, and those of course will
form the foundations for the commitments in

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:20.970
the Long-Term Plan to be delivered over the
remainder of the period. We have also incorporated

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:27.470
our assumptions in terms of continuing challenge
to all of us, in terms of running the system

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:34.080
with reduced running costs, and prioritising
funding for frontline patient care as far

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:41.860
as possible. And we’ve also made provision
for some particular risk that we face over

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:47.730
this period around income from the voluntary
and statutory schemes for regulating the prices

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:53.340
of branded medicines.
In particular, I mentioned protecting commitments

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:58.840
from the Five Year Forward View. We have also
ensured that we’ve made provision to deliver

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:04.000
on the commitments in the Long-Term Plan around
increasing the share of spend on mental health,

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.730
and also, increasing the share – the historic
commitments to increase the share of spend

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:15.270
going on both primary and medical, and community
health services over the period, and those

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:21.420
significant commitments are fully funded in
the proposals that the Board has before it

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:28.740
today.
In terms of then how – what those sort of

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.930
principles lead to in terms of an allocation
of resources, table two on page five sets

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:45.130
that out in headline terms. I think what’s
– you’ll see that overall CCG programme

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:53.190
spend is projected to grow by 5.7% in ‘19/20.
As I mentioned earlier, a significant chunk

00:12:53.190 --> 00:13:03.390
of that is to deliver funding for the pay
increases that were funded centrally in 2018/19.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:09.640
The other aspects of that table that I would
draw the Board’s attention to are the movements

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:15.930
in respect of provider support set out in
that table, so you’ll see that as we have

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:21.010
announced in the planning guidance jointly
between NHS England and NHS Improvement, there’s

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:27.991
a significant reduction in the Provider Sustainability
Fund, in part due to a transfer of some of

00:13:27.991 --> 00:13:34.700
those resources into core prices for urgent
and emergency care services. But also, to

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:39.641
reflect over the period the introduction of
a financial recovery fund, which is a significant

00:13:39.641 --> 00:13:46.510
and decisive move to provide support directly
to provider organisations who have plans in

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:54.090
place to deliver sustainable cost reductions
over the five-year period, and to eliminate

00:13:54.090 --> 00:14:01.090
operating deficits over that period, and those
movements are reflected in the allocation

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:06.720
of resources in table two.
As a result of that, of course, it’s important

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:14.350
to note that we are not holding significant
central provision for levels of deficit anywhere

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:19.920
in the system beyond that. You know, this
is a change in the approach to directly support

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:26.190
organisations who are delivering improvements
in their efficiency over the period, rather

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:32.350
than holding back large reserves centrally
in order to offset subsequent deficits.

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:37.110
So, those are the headlines in terms of the
overall disposition of resources. I’ll turn

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:44.060
now to the specifics around CCG allocations,
which, again, we’ve discussed in Board committees

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:49.350
previously. And as colleagues will know, we
work on the basis of recommendations from

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:56.140
ACRA, an independent expert committee chaired
by Professor Peter Smith, who made a series

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:03.810
of recommendations which we propose to accept
around the composition of the target formula

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:09.620
for CCG resources, and I think the Board will
wish to thank Professor Smith and all members

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:16.730
of ACRA for their advice. The annex to the
paper contains the letter from Professor Smith

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:21.020
recording those recommendations. And I’ll
just run through the headlines of that, because

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:26.140
I think there were some very important and
welcome developments that we’re able to

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:32.930
recommend to the Board this – for allocations
over this period.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:39.040
Firstly, on population, I think there are
two changes that I would draw the Board’s

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:46.650
attention to set out in paragraph 22. The
first is a change in order that we will – rather

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:51.960
than looking at the list – the size of a
sort of patient list of a general practice,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:56.420
which is the sort of building block of the
population component of the formula, at a

00:15:56.420 --> 00:16:00.733
fixed point in time, we’ll now look at the
annual average of the registered list for

00:16:00.733 --> 00:16:05.100
the most recent year, and that means that
we’ll be better picking up cyclical patterns

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:10.740
of population movements in local areas. And
again, I think that’s a welcome change.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:15.840
And we also have now from the Office of National
Statistics much more specific population projections

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:22.120
which take account of both age and gender,
and we propose to reflect those in the formula

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:28.250
going forward.
We’ve also had – we also have a big step

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:34.990
forward on the approach we take to estimating
relative need for community health services.

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:39.680
Heretofore, we haven’t had a separate formula
for community services because we haven’t

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:45.220
had the data that has allowed us to develop
one, but thanks to the commitment and hard

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:50.510
work of community services providers up and
down the land, we are now starting to get

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:55.920
early returns from the new community services
data sets, and taking those early returns

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:00.360
together with more detailed patient-level
data from a small number – from a handful

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:06.890
of local areas, we’ve been able to develop
a specific formula for relative need for community

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:10.939
health services, and again, we – based on
ACRA’s advice, we’re recommending that

00:17:10.939 --> 00:17:20.230
we adopt that formula as set out in the paper
for ‘19/20 and beyond. This in particular

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:26.652
reflects higher need for community services
in areas that have high proportions of people

00:17:26.652 --> 00:17:32.539
aged over 85, particularly in rural and coastal
CCGs when you look at the national distribution

00:17:32.539 --> 00:17:37.299
of that population, but also, importantly,
in some of the deprived older populations

00:17:37.299 --> 00:17:44.289
in the Midlands and North in particular. It’s
a big step forward to have a specific formula

00:17:44.289 --> 00:17:49.649
for community services, and – but it’s
not the end of the story, and we hope to continue

00:17:49.649 --> 00:17:54.990
work as the data quality improves, and as
we get a more comprehensive national data

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:59.990
set, we will continue our work to refine that
formula going forward.

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:06.909
We’ve also been able to make improvements
to the mental health funding. And again, the

00:18:06.909 --> 00:18:11.629
thing that’s enabled us to do that is better
data coming forward, in particular from IAPT

00:18:11.629 --> 00:18:19.691
activity collections. And again, we have – we
propose to implement those improvements to

00:18:19.691 --> 00:18:25.230
the formula. That results in higher need indices
for some coastal areas, and areas with older

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:30.110
populations, due in the main to the fact that
we now – all the work that’s gone into

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:35.670
better diagnosis and recording of dementia
is now coming through in the national data

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:40.840
and will now be reflected in the allocation
of resources.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:46.549
On health inequalities, which of course, is
something that the Board has always taken

00:18:46.549 --> 00:18:53.090
very seriously in considering the approach
it takes to the allocation of resources, we,

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:59.669
based on ACRA’s advice, are recommending
that we continue to use the same measure for

00:18:59.669 --> 00:19:05.299
the adjustments that we make for health inequalities
and unmet need. But ACRA have recommended

00:19:05.299 --> 00:19:15.350
to us improvements in the way that we translate
that data into the allocations formula, which

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:20.659
is taking better account in particular of
the areas with the most extreme levels of

00:19:20.659 --> 00:19:28.509
unmet need, the sort of outliers on the spectrum.
And again, we are proposing to accept ACRA’s

00:19:28.509 --> 00:19:32.710
recommended changes there. And it’s worth
noting that that leads to some significant

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:37.950
increases in funding for some areas of the
country – Blackpool CCG’s core target

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:47.139
allocation increases by 5.13% as set out in
paragraph 34 of the paper. But that’s – as

00:19:47.139 --> 00:19:51.600
I say, we are – on the basis of ACRA’s
advice, we’re recommending those improvements,

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:59.309
and I think, again, it’s a welcome refinement
of the approach that the Board takes to allocations.

00:19:59.309 --> 00:20:06.250
Aside from that, we are recommending that
we continue with the same weighting for the

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:09.869
health inequalities adjustment that we’ve
used in the past. That’s a 15% adjustment

00:20:09.869 --> 00:20:17.299
for primary care, a 10% adjustment for CCG-commissioned
services, and a 5% adjustment for specialised

00:20:17.299 --> 00:20:24.389
services. We have set out in the Long-Term
Plan that we will commission ACRA, the advisory

00:20:24.389 --> 00:20:28.360
committee, to conduct and publish further
work on the inequalities adjustment, so that

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:35.610
we can continue our commitments to improving
and refining the approach we take to reflecting

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:38.679
unmet need in our locations.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Can I just hold you there,

00:20:38.679 --> 00:20:43.840
Matt? Just maybe ask your – maybe Simon
just to sort of, in layman’s speak, just

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:49.600
characterise the aggregate difference about
where the – how – where the money flows,

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:55.179
and what kind of populations are going to
receive more and which will receive less.

00:20:55.179 --> 00:20:59.629
Can you sort of give a flavour?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Sure.

00:20:59.629 --> 00:21:05.049
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: So, I think the main changes
in distribution, as I said, are driven by

00:21:05.049 --> 00:21:08.610
the new community services formula and the
new mental health formula, and the changes

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:16.809
in the way we take account of inequalities.
And in terms of the impact that has in kind

00:21:16.809 --> 00:21:21.999
of – in pounds billion terms, by the end
of the period, we expect that there’ll be

00:21:21.999 --> 00:21:28.320
– it’s £2.2 billion will be redistributed
within target allocations, a move from one

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:34.030
path of distribution to another, to reflect
levels of health inequalities.

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:38.279
On the – as I said, the community services
– changes to the community services formula

00:21:38.279 --> 00:21:46.289
have in particular moved resources to areas
with higher levels of population over 85.

00:21:46.289 --> 00:21:50.970
It’s coastal areas and some rural areas
that sort of stand out from the map as having

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:59.850
benefited most significantly from those changes.
And other areas, as I say, with high levels

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:05.781
of population over 85. In the mental – on
changes in the mental health formula, what

00:22:05.781 --> 00:22:13.669
you see is, as you’d perhaps expect, that
some of the deprived inner-city areas have

00:22:13.669 --> 00:22:18.760
seen the biggest changes in their need index.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Do you want to add to that,

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.260
Simon, or…
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, everybody is gaining

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:25.500
on the back of this, because obviously, we’re
talking about a £20.5 billion real-terms

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:32.559
increase in – over – phased over five
years. What this is doing is changing the

00:22:32.559 --> 00:22:37.130
fair shares targets, and then we have a pace
of change as to how quickly we can get to

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:44.059
those targets. One of the decisions before
us this morning is, should we ensure, as of

00:22:44.059 --> 00:22:50.309
April 1st, that no part of the country is
more than 5% below its fair share formula.

00:22:50.309 --> 00:22:54.169
We’ve managed to achieve that in the past.
Obviously, a new formula means you get a new

00:22:54.169 --> 00:22:59.080
distribution of starting positions. Our proposition
is that we should move immediately to ensure

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:07.080
that everybody is within that banding, and
I think in all honesty, we can say that since

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:11.701
our – the approach to trying to allocate
funding based on need began in the National

00:23:11.701 --> 00:23:18.200
Health Service in 1976, this will be the fairest
and most precise allocation of the growing

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:24.080
resources that the NHS has ever seen.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Well that’s – thank

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:30.169
you. That’s very clear. And what we wanted
to hear, yeah, that’s absolutely right.

00:23:30.169 --> 00:23:35.400
And actually, in terms of tackling health
inequalities, this is fundamental to doing

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.509
that.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Particularly when linked

00:23:37.509 --> 00:23:44.139
to the Long-Term Plan requirements that local
areas will have to show how the extra money

00:23:44.139 --> 00:23:49.710
that is being deployed for health inequalities
is actually funding services or interventions

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:53.119
that will narrow those inequalities. It’s
one thing just to have the funding in your

00:23:53.119 --> 00:23:57.630
overall pot, but unless you can actually show
the chain of connection to what’s going

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:01.840
to make a difference, in the way that Michelle
and Victor have previously pointed out, the

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:06.102
need to hardwire inequalities reductions into
all of the local plans that people are going

00:24:06.102 --> 00:24:10.259
to be developing between now and the autumn,
as well as the national programmes. That’s

00:24:10.259 --> 00:24:14.169
the point that we’ve got to stress, and
that will be different than what has gone

00:24:14.169 --> 00:24:16.500
before.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes. Thank you. Matt, anything

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:20.909
else you wanted to add?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Simon just mentioned, as

00:24:20.909 --> 00:24:25.389
I say, that we are continuing the approach
of bringing all areas to within 5% of targets.

00:24:25.389 --> 00:24:31.830
And in fact, by the end of the period, we
are able to bring – no CCG will be more

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:39.179
than 3.5% below target by ‘23/24. I think
the only other thing I’d add in terms of

00:24:39.179 --> 00:24:45.600
the approach we’ve taken to the sort of
pace at which the new target allocations are

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:51.309
actually sort of reflected in actual allocations
on the ground, is that we’ve provided – we’ve

00:24:51.309 --> 00:24:56.309
made provisions for a higher floor allocation,
so the minimum allocation for programme growth

00:24:56.309 --> 00:25:02.909
that any CCG will receive is higher over this
five-year period than it has been in approaches

00:25:02.909 --> 00:25:07.590
we’ve taken previously, and that reflects
the sort of price pressures that are coming

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:11.779
through the system, but also, the significant
expectations in terms of new deliverables

00:25:11.779 --> 00:25:15.880
coming out of the Five Year Forward View and
the Long-Term Plan.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:21.489
And I think I can probably leave it there.
The only thing I’d add finally is, I mentioned

00:25:21.489 --> 00:25:28.480
ACRA as the advisory committee, and our gratitude
to them for their work. We are also – we

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:35.919
will be setting out ACRA’s work programme
for the next round of allocations, and I would

00:25:35.919 --> 00:25:42.799
encourage everyone across the NHS, those listening
and watching to today’s meeting, to provide

00:25:42.799 --> 00:25:48.960
input to ACRA on their future work programme,
whether it’s areas of refinement they would

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:53.730
recommend, because we want to make sure that
we are drawing on that input as we consider

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:58.539
future changes to the formula for future years.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent. Any – yeah, Richard.

00:25:58.539 --> 00:26:03.640
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Three observations, David.
First of all, I think the balance of this

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:08.320
feels right in terms of making sure everyone’s
got enough growth to deal with things that

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:13.600
we’re asking them to do, but that there
is additional growth being targeted at the

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:18.289
areas of greatest need. So, I think that difficult
balance between stability and change, I think

00:26:18.289 --> 00:26:23.169
this feels right, looking at it.
Second thing is, there’s an awful lot of

00:26:23.169 --> 00:26:27.519
change going on below this paper. If you look
at what’s happened on provider sustainability,

00:26:27.519 --> 00:26:32.220
on community services, on mental health service
and the overall fund, there’s a lot of things

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:40.500
changing here. And I hope that following on
from this, we can sort of move on from some

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:45.929
of the arguments about distribution and say,
actually, we’ve got the money sorted out

00:26:45.929 --> 00:26:50.269
distributionally broadly in the right way,
now let’s get on and think about how we

00:26:50.269 --> 00:26:53.509
actually use it rather than spend too much
more time arguing about distribution.

00:26:53.509 --> 00:27:01.830
And the third thing on the provider side,
I welcome the shift towards sort of recognising

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:06.619
what we need to put in realistically to give
the providers chance to deliver the things

00:27:06.619 --> 00:27:13.210
that we need them to do. I welcome the fact
that we’re not holding back contingencies

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:19.749
just to release later on in the year when
problems emerge. But it’s important, conversely

00:27:19.749 --> 00:27:24.859
on that, that actually, people then deliver
against realistic plans, because if they don’t,

00:27:24.859 --> 00:27:31.789
all we can do is go back and cut someone else.
So, I think the balance, the deal, on saying,

00:27:31.789 --> 00:27:37.610
‘We’ve set you realistic expectations
from you, but actually, now we’ve really

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:46.989
got to make sure we deliver on those’.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, that point is very well

00:27:46.989 --> 00:27:51.119
made. Anyone else got anything to raise? Well,
I think –

00:27:51.119 --> 00:27:53.340
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Can I just add, we’ve obviously
targeted this quite extensively at the commissioning

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:58.429
committee over the last quarter of last year,
and I felt – I just want to commend the

00:27:58.429 --> 00:28:03.929
team for – both the team and ACRA for really
moving on and modernising NHS in terms of

00:28:03.929 --> 00:28:08.369
accepting – looking at large-scale data,
thinking about how we address deprivation

00:28:08.369 --> 00:28:10.830
and things. So, I think really well done by
the team.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:17.620
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. It’s one of those rather
arcane parts of medicine, which are – or

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:24.099
of a care system, which is hugely important.
So, Matt, thank you very much, and thanks

00:28:24.099 --> 00:28:29.210
– so we can approve the paper? Move on?
Yeah, good. Thank you very much.

00:28:29.210 --> 00:28:33.590
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I’ve used £0.5 trillion
of public money that we’ve just allocated

00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:38.409
– yeah.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Excellent. Ian, I think

00:28:38.409 --> 00:28:41.850
we’re going to move on to the – where
you’ve got to with the GP contract, and

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:46.539
will you introduce Richard for us, please?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Great, thank you very much, Chair,

00:28:46.539 --> 00:28:53.619
and welcome, Dr Richard Vautrey, who is the
chair of the BMA General Practitioners Committee

00:28:53.619 --> 00:29:01.889
in England. So, last summer, when Richard
and I started discussions, we had a choice.

00:29:01.889 --> 00:29:06.109
We could either continue the annual round
of contract discussions, or we could be a

00:29:06.109 --> 00:29:12.340
bit more imaginative and think bigger, taking
advantage of the new five-year funding settlement.

00:29:12.340 --> 00:29:19.019
And we’ve opted for the latter course, and
today, we bring a comprehensive deal, we bring

00:29:19.019 --> 00:29:25.019
it together, that’s been jointly developed
and agreed by GPC England, as well as by the

00:29:25.019 --> 00:29:30.690
government, for the Board’s ratification.
It marks the first major step to implement

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:36.279
the Long-Term Plan, as well as dealing with
many of the big challenges currently facing

00:29:36.279 --> 00:29:40.279
general practice. We cover a lot of ground,
and I’m just going to run through some of

00:29:40.279 --> 00:29:44.649
the highlights before I turn to Richard and
then your questions.

00:29:44.649 --> 00:29:55.279
So, what does it include? Funding entitlements
for 20,000 additional staff. Just as the GP

00:29:55.279 --> 00:30:02.779
charter, in 1965, led to practice nurses becoming
a core part of the general practice team,

00:30:02.779 --> 00:30:09.460
so now will pharmacists, paramedics, physios,
social prescribers and physicians associates.

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:16.580
70% reimbursement, 100% for social prescribing
link workers. No taper, unlike the current

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:26.289
pharmacist scheme. Up to £891 million in
dedicated new funding by ‘23/24. An extension

00:30:26.289 --> 00:30:34.799
of measures to recruit and retain more GPs
and nurses. A new fellowship scheme covering

00:30:34.799 --> 00:30:43.309
both an early and full response to the recently
published partnership review covering five

00:30:43.309 --> 00:30:50.749
of their second recommendations. We explicitly
ask government for a partial pension scheme

00:30:50.749 --> 00:30:58.269
like the 50% scheme that exists for local
government. Recruitment and retention made

00:30:58.269 --> 00:31:05.580
easier, because of the new NHS resolution
indemnity scheme, starting in April this year.

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:11.519
It will be free at the point of membership.
All practice staff and out-of-hours staff

00:31:11.519 --> 00:31:17.230
will be covered, including sessional GPs and
all the new workers I’ve already alluded

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:24.499
to. And it’s without a cut in income as
many had feared, but instead, a 1.4% rise

00:31:24.499 --> 00:31:32.580
in core practice income in 2019/20.
Major improvements to the Quality and Outcomes

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:40.320
Framework. 31% of the least effective indicators
are being retired. New quality improvement

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:46.259
modules to support professional development,
starting with prescribing safety and end-of-life

00:31:46.259 --> 00:31:54.080
care. Indicator reform, starting with diabetes
and hypertension, and an overhaul of exception

00:31:54.080 --> 00:32:01.259
reporting. There’ll be an entitlement to
a network contract with 100% of England covered

00:32:01.259 --> 00:32:10.861
by 1st July this year, backed by cash and
development support from CCGs and ICSs. Networks

00:32:10.861 --> 00:32:20.849
will become the basis for all NHS local care
services. There will be up to £1.799 billion

00:32:20.849 --> 00:32:25.539
that could flow through networks under this
national deal by ‘23/24.

00:32:25.539 --> 00:32:35.600
Continued IT infrastructure support. New digital
platform support, to enable patients to have

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:41.970
the right to web and video consultations.
There’s recognition of the burden of subject

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:50.649
access requests. Data protection officer support.
Fairer funding to avoid unwarranted redistribution,

00:32:50.649 --> 00:32:57.320
given the emergence of digital-first models.
An out-of-area registration review. Joining

00:32:57.320 --> 00:33:04.629
up the urgent care system outside hospital,
with networks taking on extended hours. 111

00:33:04.629 --> 00:33:10.019
redirecting more people to pharmacists, we
expect than it will to practices, to help

00:33:10.019 --> 00:33:16.169
alleviate demand. The extra capacity will
help networks deliver improvements for their

00:33:16.169 --> 00:33:23.669
patients in a phased way. Working with community
health teams. This includes medication reviews,

00:33:23.669 --> 00:33:29.249
better support for care homes, implementing
the vanguard model. Proactive care for people

00:33:29.249 --> 00:33:34.869
with complex needs, including people with
multi-morbidity. Supporting earlier cancer

00:33:34.869 --> 00:33:44.179
diagnosis. And if you just think, the average
network of 50,000 people sees typically 270

00:33:44.179 --> 00:33:50.710
to 280 new cancer diagnoses a year. The Long-Term
Plan goal is we move from roughly 50% diagnosis

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:57.570
at stage I and stage II up to 75% by 2028.
So, for every network, that’s an extra 70

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:04.970
or so people to be diagnosed at stage I and
stage II. Working closely with secondary care

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:09.570
services and the development of the rapid
diagnostic centres, as well as the review

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:16.750
of screening that Professor Sir Mike Richards
is undertaking at the moment. Also, earlier

00:34:16.750 --> 00:34:22.910
detection of the many people in England who
have hypertension and atrial fibrillation

00:34:22.910 --> 00:34:28.320
who are currently undiagnosed, as well as
picking up on our last conversation: local

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:33.920
action at the network level within neighbourhoods,
to tackle health inequalities.

00:34:33.920 --> 00:34:40.410
It will also involve implementation by networks
of the NHS comprehensive model of personalised

00:34:40.410 --> 00:34:45.930
care, which we turn to as our next agenda
item. There will be a new data service for

00:34:45.930 --> 00:34:52.860
networks to show how they’re doing on prevention
goals, on access, on proactive care and wider

00:34:52.860 --> 00:35:00.680
NHS use. A new £300 million fund by ‘23/24.
An investment and impact fund to help secure

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:06.110
faster progress on all of these Long-Term
Plan goals, including the redesign of outpatient

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:13.250
care. And a programme of testing changes before
they’re introduced. This five-year deal

00:35:13.250 --> 00:35:20.650
provides funding certainty for the first time
covering five years in primary care history.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:31.360
Core practice income will rise by £978 million
by ‘23/24; that’s 12% in cash terms, and

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:39.610
an average anticipated 0.4% in real terms,
with then the £1.799 billion for primary

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:45.860
care network funding on top of that. And there
will also be a balancing mechanism to protect

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:53.730
against either excessive unanticipated inflation,
or practice earnings.

00:35:53.730 --> 00:36:00.340
So, before I hand over to Richard, I’d just
like to say that the progress I think we’ve

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:07.050
made over the last month is down to the quality
of the joint working between our teams, Richard?

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:12.500
And I’d like to pay tribute to your side,
to Farah, to Krishna and Mark, as well as

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:19.270
yourself, and then my deputy chair Ed Waller,
who’s sitting in the audience there, who’s

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:25.550
done an absolutely outstanding job, as has
his team, Amanda Doyle, Cathy Winfield, two

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:30.490
of our ICS leaders; Abid Irfan; and Nikki
Kanani, who is also in the audience.

00:36:30.490 --> 00:36:37.990
&gt;&gt;Richard Vautrey: Thank you very much, Ian.
Thank you for inviting me. I think this is

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:42.260
a really important moment for general practice.
I think it’s backed and was made possible

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:46.180
by the commitment that NHS England made around
the funding, which you’ve just been talking

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:52.400
about, for general practice, primary care,
and community care services. And I think it’s

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:56.870
fundamental that we shore up the foundation
of the NHS, because general practice is the

00:36:56.870 --> 00:37:01.320
bedrock on which the rest of the NHS is built,
and we want a strong foundation so that the

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:07.140
rest of the NHS can thrive and develop. And
this has been a long gestation; I think we’ve

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:11.180
worked really hard to get where we’ve got
to. You can get a sense of how comprehensive

00:37:11.180 --> 00:37:16.460
the scale and scope is from what Ian has just
outlined. But I think there are some fundamental

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:22.190
things here which I just wanted to highlight.
The first is that this will allow a workforce

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:28.090
expansion. You’ll have seen the headlines
today, around 20,000-odd multi-professionals

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:34.170
working alongside and embedded with GPs in
their practices. So, it will be pharmacists,

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:41.160
social prescribers, physicians associates,
physios and others, working with GPs to provide

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:45.920
better care to their patients, and recognising
the fact – something which I have and my

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:50.540
colleagues have articulated repeatedly over
recent years – the workload pressures that

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:56.060
GPs are under will in part be helped and supported
by this expansion of the workforce team. And

00:37:56.060 --> 00:38:01.430
I think that’s fundamental to enable patients
to get an appointment with their GP and others

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:06.370
within the team. And that’s what we want
to see change, is to help practices support

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:10.850
their workload pressures and to do more in
different ways through the digital changes

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:14.210
that will be taking place, as well. So, I
think this is a fundamental change.

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:19.990
I think something else to point out is that
this builds on the existing GMS contract and

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:26.450
the practice that has stood the test of time
for many, many years. Ian alludes to 1965,

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:32.110
another landmark time, and there’s been
various times along the journey for general

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:37.120
practice, but I think this feels like another
watershed moment, and provides us the opportunity,

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:42.360
if implemented well, as we hope it will be,
and we’ll certainly be committed to do that,

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:47.130
to actually make a real change that not only
delivers those 20,000 additional staff, but

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:51.730
also provides a different environment within
general practice that gives confidence to

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:57.330
young doctors to becomes GPs, and for older
doctors to stay as GPs, so that we can actually

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:02.810
expand the workforce as well. Because crucially,
we do need more GPs alongside these other

00:39:02.810 --> 00:39:06.470
healthcare professionals working alongside
us, to deliver what patients expect us to

00:39:06.470 --> 00:39:09.450
do.
And I think thirdly, I think the real commitment

00:39:09.450 --> 00:39:14.860
to deliver on indemnity changes, to actually
move us to a situation that has long been

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:20.170
the case in secondary care, where doctors
are not penalised for choosing general practice

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:27.140
as a career choice, and having to pay significantly
inflated and rapidly increasing amounts of

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:32.430
indemnity costs just to practice as a GP.
That will be taken away by this change, and

00:39:32.430 --> 00:39:37.120
that will make a fundamental difference both
for younger doctors and older doctors as they

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:39.110
choose to be general practitioners in the
future.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:45.270
So, I think this is an important moment. I
want to pay tribute, I think, to Ian and to

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:48.710
his team, particularly to Ed, who’s done
a huge amount of work in terms of getting

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:53.740
us to this point, and to the teams that worked
with you. And you’ve already mentioned sort

00:39:53.740 --> 00:39:58.210
of my team as well who’ve worked with us
so diligently, so that Mark and Farah and

00:39:58.210 --> 00:40:03.800
Krish, and the support teams within BMA who’ve
made this possible. There is still a lot of

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:09.370
work to do. We should not give the sense that
the problems will be solved on 1st April when

00:40:09.370 --> 00:40:13.560
this contract change starts to be implemented.
This is going to be a long journey. There’ll

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:17.950
be bumps on the road. We want to work with
you in a collaborative way, a way that we’ve

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:22.440
been able to do over the last few months,
to make this is a reality and to make general

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:25.750
practice and the rest of the NHS better as
a result of doing so.

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:29.790
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you both very much. Why
don’t we just ask you some questions, if

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:34.000
we can? I mean, if Ed and Nikki would like
to say anything as well, I mean, come up and

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:37.990
sit next to Richard there, and you can give
us your comments, as well. We’d like to

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:41.410
hear them.
But anyway, while we wait for you to come

00:40:41.410 --> 00:40:44.200
forward if you’d like to – don’t feel
you have to, but you may now feel you have

00:40:44.200 --> 00:40:49.190
to…
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: You have to bring your own chair.

00:40:49.190 --> 00:40:53.670
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Let’s open it up for questions
anyway, from the Board. Who’d like to kick

00:40:53.670 --> 00:40:57.977
off? I think Ruth, did you want to kick off?
I should say that this was your first meeting,

00:40:57.977 --> 00:40:59.200
I think, with the Board, wasn’t it? So,
I should have said that at the beginning,

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:01.850
but welcome.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I’ve used £0.5 trillion

00:41:01.850 --> 00:41:04.880
of public money that we’ve just allocated
– yeah.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:09.910
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: A couple of things, and thank
you both for that presentation of this work,

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:15.330
so thank you. I’m delighted this builds
on the work that NHS England have been doing

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:20.750
over a number of years to see the number of
practice nurses increase by over 600, so that’s

00:41:20.750 --> 00:41:25.470
fantastic. It’s good as well that we’re
recognising that there is a practice team.

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:31.780
It’s not just about doctors and nurses,
and I think this particular work that you’ve

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:41.160
all done takes us to the next level of multi-professional
teams coming together for patients, and I

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:44.830
think that’s fantastic.
I’d be keen, I guess, to work with you about

00:41:44.830 --> 00:41:51.390
how we really celebrate the primary care fellowship
programme for newly qualified registered nurses

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:56.340
and doctors, and I’m just wondering how
you think I can support, in my new role as

00:41:56.340 --> 00:42:02.390
Chief Nursing Officer, how we ensure that
there are as many nurses going for that as

00:42:02.390 --> 00:42:03.710
doctors.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nikki?

00:42:03.710 --> 00:42:08.120
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Thank you. I really do recognise
that. I think it’s great that we’re talking

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:12.280
about the wider team now, and it’s the only
way we’re going to work and keep general

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:16.010
practice sustainable. Actually, your team’s
already been in touch, so we’ve started

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:20.020
to do some work understanding which fellowships,
because there are some nursing fellowships

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:23.540
that exist around the country, as you know,
so we’re going to try and get the best out

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:28.950
of those examples and build up our nursing
fellowships to reflect that, and then I’m

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:32.650
coming back to you in a couple of weeks’
time to present it back to you and see what

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:33.650
you think.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Fantastic, superb. Thank you very

00:42:33.650 --> 00:42:34.650
much. Look forward to supporting it personally.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Let’s go to Steve first,

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:35.650
and then Michelle. Steve?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I’ve used £0.5 trillion

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:36.650
of public money that we’ve just allocated
– yeah.

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:43.360
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: So, I know how much hard
work has gone into this, so again, my congratulations.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:48.860
I think it’s a huge step forward, and I
think it will be seen as a landmark moment

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:53.390
in terms of our support and development of
primary care. So, like Ruth, I think it’s

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:59.650
fantastic that we are recognising, again,
the wider team in primary care that supports

00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:06.000
general practice. It’s there already, but
this gives it another further push. And like

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:11.680
David, I have visited many practices where
I have a sense of seeing the future, where

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:17.050
many of – much of this work is already going
on, where pharmacists are being used, and

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:22.770
others, in really innovative ways. But I just
wondered whether Richard and Nikki, as practising

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:28.810
GPs, could give a real sense as to what this
means for a member of the public. So, Richard,

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:36.110
you talked about being able to get more time
with the GP, but it’s not just that, so

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:41.830
I think it would be very useful for the Board
to hear from the public and citizen and patient

00:43:41.830 --> 00:43:46.351
perspective what this will mean.
&gt;&gt;Richard Vautrey: Yeah, thanks, Steve. As

00:43:46.351 --> 00:43:54.250
a GP in Leeds, I’ve had the fortune to have
a CCG who supported some of these, employing

00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:58.830
a pharmacist in recent months, who’s made
a tremendous difference to our practice, not

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:03.920
only sort of in reducing some of the workload
burden for the individual GPs, but in medicine

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:09.590
reconciliation, liaising sort of with local
community pharmacists, and with the hospital

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:13.840
sort of when patients have been discharged,
but also liaising directly with individual

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:18.450
patients, patients with complex problems,
people who have been addicted to opioids and

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:25.250
other medication sort of that they want help
and support to get off. And with the pharmacist’s

00:44:25.250 --> 00:44:28.630
direct supervision, they have been able to
do that in a way that’s made a big difference

00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:34.270
to our patients. Similarly, we’ve had for
the last few months Physio First contacts,

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:38.740
so when a patient rings up with back pain
or knee pain, then they can be put in touch

00:44:38.740 --> 00:44:44.430
with a physio first of all rather than necessarily
having to see a GP and then be referred to

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:48.410
that physio to get further advice.
And over the winter, we’ve been fortunate

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:53.260
to be working with a scheme around paramedics,
so we’ve had paramedics doing home visits.

00:44:53.260 --> 00:44:57.830
And so where it’s been appropriate, the
patient has been triaged first by a GP following

00:44:57.830 --> 00:45:02.260
a home visit request. The paramedic has been
to do the visit and then reported back to

00:45:02.260 --> 00:45:07.420
the practice, so again, it just frees up time
and it provides a sense of working as a comprehensive

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:11.401
team. So, we’ve seen the reality of what
can happen. What we want to ensure is, every

00:45:11.401 --> 00:45:16.040
practice has a pharmacist in place. Every
practice has the ability to work with a social

00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:20.010
prescriber, and others as well, because I
think by doing so, it will start to magnify

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:21.420
some of the benefits.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nikki?

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:24.850
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Okay, I mean, I can’t add
much more to that. I won’t steal James’s

00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:29.700
thunder for later, but I mean, I’ve talked
about my social prescriber and how I have

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:33.650
– I mean, one of my favourite patients,
I don’t know if you’re supposed to say

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:38.120
that, but one of my favourite patients who
for years has really struggled with anxiety

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:42.200
and being really lonely in her 80s has started
tap-dancing, and before Christmas, she came

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:45.861
in to tell me that she’d fallen in love
with someone at her tap-dancing class. It

00:45:45.861 --> 00:45:49.960
has changed her life, and it means that it
changes my life, in the way that I practise,

00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:53.530
as well. But patients will get a different
kind of digital offer, as well, so my kids

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:57.280
want to access care and will want to access
care in a completely different way from that

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:04.010
lady will, and this is the beginning of being
able to do that properly.

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:08.080
&gt;&gt;David Prior: David? I think your question
was asked, yeah.

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:11.040
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, Richard and Nikki, and
the team, I think it’s great to hear from

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:15.080
you the practical examples, and there are
clearly examples across the country where

00:46:15.080 --> 00:46:23.550
this is working. I guess my question is, I’m
curious to know, how do we implement this

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:29.150
and how do we make sure that we don’t have
every practice repeating the learnings that

00:46:29.150 --> 00:46:34.690
you’ve already seen and heard? So, the paper’s
reasonably silent on what happens next, and

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:41.340
I’d be interested to know.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Shall I just pick up first on

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:48.890
that? So, this builds on a – I think a long
tradition of collaborative provision. So,

00:46:48.890 --> 00:46:57.610
just to pick another date, the 1995 out-of-hours
development deal that was done, where actually,

00:46:57.610 --> 00:47:03.930
general practice rose very rapidly to the
opportunity of the deal that the BMA did then

00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:09.630
to construct co-ops, and I think that’s
a really good precedent. We know from the

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:15.500
work that Dominic Hardy and his team are doing
that 88% of the – of practices according

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:21.900
to internal NHS England data as of end of
September are already forming primary care

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:28.280
networks, so this is not a journey that starts
kind of de novo now; this has actually been

00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:34.440
a journey that people have been on for several
years. There’s a lot of CCG support available.

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:42.720
There has been both cash support and in kind.
We encourage both. The cash support is important:

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:50.100
the £1.50 a head, plus also additional funding
to support the clinical director. I think

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:55.650
by being really clear in a way that hasn’t
been true for GP federations up until now

00:47:55.650 --> 00:48:00.510
about the money over a five-year period, actually,
it will give people the confidence to be able

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:08.600
to engage in this really fully. Additionally,
we have a major new development programme,

00:48:08.600 --> 00:48:14.080
which Dom will be running, around primary
care network development. That will be centrally

00:48:14.080 --> 00:48:19.770
funded, and it is on top of the numbers we’ve
alluded to in this particular deal. Notwithstanding

00:48:19.770 --> 00:48:25.960
that, it will clearly take some time before
all networks are equally effective across

00:48:25.960 --> 00:48:31.980
the country, and we are trying to recognise
that with the phased introduction of some

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:38.540
of the different services. The strength of
networks will partly depend on the success

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:44.740
they have in being able to recruit the different
additional staff, but I think people can potentially

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:53.220
see, you know, this is maybe somewhere between
16 and 20 whole-time equivalents per network

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:59.511
additionally coming in by ‘23/24, and that
will make a really major difference. I don’t

00:48:59.511 --> 00:49:00.511
know if you want to add?
&gt;&gt;Richard Vautrey: Well, I think the other

00:49:00.511 --> 00:49:03.900
thing to add is that the one thing that GPs
are is world experts at responding quickly

00:49:03.900 --> 00:49:11.260
to change. So, we’ve had countless changes
in recent years, from out of hours to fund-holding,

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:17.510
to practice-based commissioning, PCG and CCG
developments, and in every case, GPs have

00:49:17.510 --> 00:49:21.370
stepped up and got on with it. I think the
difference here is, this is about provision.

00:49:21.370 --> 00:49:25.670
This is about providers working together,
and GPs are very much up for that. Sort of,

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:31.640
they want to be able to rebuild the primary
care team around their practices within their

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:35.050
localities, and I think that’s something
that’s fundamental, sort of, that will enable

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:40.090
them to be leaders of a wider community team,
which will sort of reap dividends in all sorts

00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:42.400
of ways.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think alongside that, Richard,

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:46.520
there clearly is also the digital dynamic,
as well, which will perhaps be slightly newer

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.840
to federations.
&gt;&gt;Richard Vautrey: Yes, absolutely. And I

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:52.680
think one of the fundamentals to that is ensuring
that the building blocks are in place. So,

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:58.600
one of the key lines within this is ensuring
that, you know, broadband capacity, that the

00:49:58.600 --> 00:50:02.810
kit is in place, so that then, people can
use that as a springboard to make the rest

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:08.660
of the system work. You know, if you give
kit to – and systems in place that facilitate

00:50:08.660 --> 00:50:13.050
good care, GPs will want to use it, and as
quickly as possible, but we need those fundamentals

00:50:13.050 --> 00:50:16.430
sort of in place, and we know that CCGs are
starting to roll that out now.

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:19.280
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nikki?
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: I’d just suggest to the

00:50:19.280 --> 00:50:22.800
Board that in terms of implementation, one
of the best things we can do is not to ruin

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:27.060
it before it starts. So, actually, internally,
we need to make sure that we join the dots

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:31.370
on primary care, make sure we know where we’re
trying to get to, and then let the networks

00:50:31.370 --> 00:50:35.820
get on. This is building on the social movement.
People are doing this anyway because they

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:39.720
know it’s the right thing to do. They want
to do it for their patients and their population.

00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:43.100
They are absolutely addressing the health
inequalities that we talked about that Simon

00:50:43.100 --> 00:50:47.460
referred to, so we need to let that happen,
and then step in, escalate and solve when

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:51.450
it’s appropriate for us to do so.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ed, do you want to comment?

00:50:51.450 --> 00:50:55.890
&gt;&gt;Ed Waller: I guess the last thing I would
say is probably that there is already some

00:50:55.890 --> 00:51:00.510
learning on some aspects of this from things
like the existing clinical pharmacist scheme,

00:51:00.510 --> 00:51:06.060
so some of that will be fed through into the
future. And explicitly, we described ways

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:10.070
in which we will go about testing some of
the things that we want to do through the

00:51:10.070 --> 00:51:13.870
contract over the next five years, and having
a five-year contract gives us the opportunity

00:51:13.870 --> 00:51:17.000
to do that in way we haven’t been able to,
because we can see what comes further down

00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:20.270
the track, and we can make sure by the time
we get there, we’ve collectively tested

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:27.060
how best to do some of these things.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nikki, if you had ten F2 doctors

00:51:27.060 --> 00:51:28.390
sitting in front of you –
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Yes.

00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:32.030
&gt;&gt;David Prior: – and they were thinking,
‘Shall we go into general practice or go

00:51:32.030 --> 00:51:36.150
into acute medicine’, what would you say?
How would you – what would you – what

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:39.550
would be your sales pitch to them?
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: It’s funny. I’ve been

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:43.820
doing this quite a lot recently, you wouldn’t
be surprised to hear. Do you know, when I

00:51:43.820 --> 00:51:48.740
ask people if they want to go into general
practice, very few hands are put up. I’ve

00:51:48.740 --> 00:51:52.650
had over 100 messages this morning from people
in the profession – that’s trainees, that’s

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:56.610
medical students, that’s nurses, that’s
clinical pharmacists – saying, this is the

00:51:56.610 --> 00:52:00.810
moment, this is absolutely the moment where
we can see that this is going to be different,

00:52:00.810 --> 00:52:03.910
we’re going to look after you, we’re going
to value you, we’re going to respect you,

00:52:03.910 --> 00:52:07.520
and we’re going to make primary care absolutely
take the role that it should do within the

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:11.121
wider system, to make the whole system work.
So, they will be engaged and they’ll want

00:52:11.121 --> 00:52:15.460
to be part of this.
&gt;&gt;Richard Vautrey: No, I mean, I would echo

00:52:15.460 --> 00:52:19.530
that. I mean, as I said earlier, I think this
does provide a real confidence boost to the

00:52:19.530 --> 00:52:22.930
profession as a whole we clearly need to see
implemented. And there’ll be lots of cynics

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:26.840
around. They’ve seen false dawns before.
I think there is a significant investment

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:30.330
here. There’s a real commitment around the
workforce, you know, and I think that will

00:52:30.330 --> 00:52:33.820
start to make a difference. But, you know,
as Nikki said, we need to be really careful

00:52:33.820 --> 00:52:37.840
not to sort of – you know, to overplay things.
We need to sort of be measured in our approach.

00:52:37.840 --> 00:52:42.690
But certainly, GPs want to provide better
care to their patients with the right support,

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:47.330
and we’re up for that.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Maybe just conclude that, after

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:53.250
you, Simon, just to sort of set this in the
context of the Long-Term Plan and how it sits

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:57.020
within that.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, the Long-Term Plan

00:52:57.020 --> 00:53:02.480
really has two big thrusts, doesn’t it.
One is changing the way services work together

00:53:02.480 --> 00:53:07.760
for patients, and the other is using that
to drive big improvements in clinical outcomes

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:12.450
for cancer and heart disease, and mental health,
and the other big killers and disablers of

00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:16.960
the population. And the foundation of both
of those is primary care, because we have

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:23.650
300 million patient visits a year to GP practices,
you know, compared, say, with 15 million visits

00:53:23.650 --> 00:53:28.500
to hospital A&amp;E departments. So, as Richard
said, primary care is absolutely essential.

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:33.640
It’s been under real stress as a result
of funding pressures versus workload increases.

00:53:33.640 --> 00:53:40.740
We’ve had, as a result, retirements, but
the flipside is, we’ve seen an increasing

00:53:40.740 --> 00:53:46.320
number of young doctors choosing general practice
as a career, so what this I think means is

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:52.590
that we’ve got the primary care and the
community health services in a given geography,

00:53:52.590 --> 00:53:55.970
in towns and cities and villages, working
closely together now – because we hadn’t

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:58.640
thought so much about that, but it’s actually
the primary care network that’s bringing

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:03.470
the community health nurses as well as the
primary care teams together – for their

00:54:03.470 --> 00:54:07.390
local people. And frankly, most people would
expect the Health Service to be operating

00:54:07.390 --> 00:54:12.220
in this joined-up way anyway. As we all know,
the history is, it hasn’t been, and by making

00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:15.760
a start with the backing for the staff and
the new ways of working, and the new offer

00:54:15.760 --> 00:54:21.030
for the public, I think people will, on a
phased basis, as Richard says, begin to really

00:54:21.030 --> 00:54:24.070
see the difference.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent. Well, thank you.

00:54:24.070 --> 00:54:28.940
I think we’re all in violent agreement.
It’s a great bit of work, Ian, so thanks

00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:29.940
to you and all your team –
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: – to formally ratify the agreement?

00:54:29.940 --> 00:54:31.870
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: I want to tell everybody.

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:36.060
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Nikki’s already tweeted
that out.

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:45.690
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: I did live tweet it. Okay,
no, thank you very much, yeah.

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:52.690
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Okay, thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, moving on to the next item,

00:54:52.690 --> 00:54:59.330
which is very closely related, on personalised
care. Ian, it’s over to you again.

00:54:59.330 --> 00:55:08.551
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, and can
I welcome James Sanderson, who is the director

00:55:08.551 --> 00:55:14.880
of the personalised care group. And Robyn,
who works very closely with James as one of

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:24.120
the members of our coproduction team.
So, from the – one part of chapter one of

00:55:24.120 --> 00:55:33.150
the Long-Term Plan to another part, this item
is about how we’re now going to implement

00:55:33.150 --> 00:55:40.560
the Long-Term Plan’s commitment on personalised
care. We set out some big goals here. 2.5

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:51.420
million people benefiting by 2023/24. We’ve
said 1,000 extra social prescribers in primary

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:57.730
care networks by 2021. In practice, as we’ve
just heard, there will be thousands of extra

00:55:57.730 --> 00:56:05.541
social prescribing link workers, funded 100%
as part of the 20,000 additional staff. These

00:56:05.541 --> 00:56:11.770
will support an estimated minimum of 900,000
people benefiting from social prescribing

00:56:11.770 --> 00:56:18.700
by 2024. 200,000 people benefiting from personal
health budgets, and we’ll hear from Robyn

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:27.570
shortly about her own particular story. I
think this is the most ambitious set of commitments

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:37.150
around personalised approaches to care made
anywhere in a healthcare system, and I think

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:43.300
it will have a profound effect not just in
the next five years, but actually, over the

00:56:43.300 --> 00:56:53.920
next 20 years. What James has done, I think,
apart from fomenting a social movement, is

00:56:53.920 --> 00:56:59.480
actually creating the rigour and structure
to be able to implement this at scale, and

00:56:59.480 --> 00:57:01.980
he will now run through the detail of our
plan.

00:57:01.980 --> 00:57:05.650
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: Thank you, Ian, thank you,
Chair. It’s great to be here today to present

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:11.420
universal personalised care. But just to start
off, in terms of what we mean by personalised

00:57:11.420 --> 00:57:15.240
care, we mean that we’re going to increase
the number of people that will have choice

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:20.320
and control over the way in which their care
is planned and delivered, around what matters

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:26.740
to them, and building on their individual
strengths and needs. So, it’s about a shift

00:57:26.740 --> 00:57:32.710
in focus from what’s wrong with you to what
matters to you. And looking at an aim to change

00:57:32.710 --> 00:57:36.901
the way in which people both interact with
their own health and care, and also the way

00:57:36.901 --> 00:57:42.910
in which they interact with the system that’s
delivering that care to them. And we aim to

00:57:42.910 --> 00:57:48.840
do this through the delivery of our comprehensive
model for personalised care. The important

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:52.930
thing about the comprehensive model is that
it builds on the significant amount of work

00:57:52.930 --> 00:57:57.810
that’s taken place across the country over
many years. But what it does is, it brings

00:57:57.810 --> 00:58:05.900
together what currently is quite a fragmented
approach, and puts six evidence-based enablers

00:58:05.900 --> 00:58:12.640
together in one model.
So, just to set out those six items, we start

00:58:12.640 --> 00:58:17.320
with shared decision-making. We want to increase
the opportunity for people to have shared

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:23.000
conversations between themselves and healthcare
professionals, looking at making decisions

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:27.700
based on the evidence and the impact of the
particular decisions that are available to

00:58:27.700 --> 00:58:32.930
them. We then want to make good on our commitments
to give people choice and control over the

00:58:32.930 --> 00:58:38.290
pathways that are available. Going beyond
that, for people with multi-morbidity and

00:58:38.290 --> 00:58:43.250
complex needs, we want to make sure that increasingly,
people have an holistic personalised care

00:58:43.250 --> 00:58:48.610
and support plan, the opportunity for them
to document a care plan that is meaningful

00:58:48.610 --> 00:58:54.050
to them and supports them, and we’re increasingly
looking to support people who are living with

00:58:54.050 --> 00:58:58.110
cancer, people with dementia and people at
the end of life care, with a really comprehensive

00:58:58.110 --> 00:59:01.700
plan for how they’re going to be supported
in the system.

00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:07.080
Beyond that, we want to look at new ways of
supporting people, and firstly, as we’ve

00:59:07.080 --> 00:59:12.680
heard earlier through social prescribing,
linking people to community-based interventions.

00:59:12.680 --> 00:59:17.960
And the opportunity to increase the number
of link workers is a really significant one.

00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:21.130
We’re building on some great work that’s
happening at the moment across the country.

00:59:21.130 --> 00:59:25.910
We’ve got voluntary-sector partners, local
government and the NHS working together with

00:59:25.910 --> 00:59:30.700
people and communities to build active communities
around social prescribing and create opportunities

00:59:30.700 --> 00:59:36.600
for people. This is an additional workforce.
It’s not to step on the toes or replicate

00:59:36.600 --> 00:59:40.800
or replacement anybody else that’s working
in the system, and link workers will work

00:59:40.800 --> 00:59:45.900
very closely with other professionals such
as social workers and occupational therapists

00:59:45.900 --> 00:59:50.040
to support people in the community.
We also want to increase the skills, knowledge

00:59:50.040 --> 00:59:55.440
and confidence of individuals, to give people
the opportunity to take a better and more

00:59:55.440 --> 00:59:59.470
active role in their health and care. We know
that some people would like to take more responsibility

00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:04.520
for their health but don’t have the means
to do so, so actually, we can support tackling

01:00:04.520 --> 01:00:09.651
health inequality by increasing that capability
that people have to self-manage, and we want

01:00:09.651 --> 01:00:14.920
to increase the opportunity for health coaching,
the opportunity for patient activation to

01:00:14.920 --> 01:00:20.030
improve that position for people. We also
want to increase our personal health budgets.

01:00:20.030 --> 01:00:24.280
We know that, when delivered well, personal
health budgets can increase the opportunity

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:29.240
for people to achieve their outcomes for independent
living and for their health and wellbeing.

01:00:29.240 --> 01:00:33.700
We know that 86% of people who receive a personal
health budget are satisfied that it meets

01:00:33.700 --> 01:00:37.380
their needs, and we’ve also got growing
evidence of the impact on the system as well,

01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:41.760
with a 17% cost saving against the cost of
delivering continuing healthcare funding by

01:00:41.760 --> 01:00:47.300
giving people that choice and control.
The important thing about universal personalised

01:00:47.300 --> 01:00:51.970
care is that it’s been coproduced over about
three years now. We’ve worked extensively

01:00:51.970 --> 01:00:57.250
with communities, with people with lived experience,
right from the beginning of this process.

01:00:57.250 --> 01:01:04.890
And the roll-out of the programme means that
we can continue to spread that best practice

01:01:04.890 --> 01:01:09.350
that’s grown up in individual areas across
the rest of the country. And what the document

01:01:09.350 --> 01:01:14.490
does, it brings together really clear operating
models for each of these components. It’s

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:19.840
not just a number of warm words related to
personalised care; it is an active delivery

01:01:19.840 --> 01:01:24.680
plan that will actually make this happen across
the country and reach that 2.5 million people.

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:29.900
I’m really pleased today to have Robyn,
who’s going to share her reflections on

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:34.160
why personalised care’s important. Robyn’s
a member of our strategic coproduction group,

01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:38.680
and sits on our advisory board for personalised
care, and has played an instrumental role,

01:01:38.680 --> 01:01:42.050
along with many others across the country,
in developing the plans that we’ve got today.

01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:46.790
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Thank you, James, thank
you, Chair. So, my name’s Robyn Chappell.

01:01:46.790 --> 01:01:51.390
I’ve been a personal health budget holder
for about three years. I had a spinal cord

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:57.240
injury in 2006, and I needed 24-hour care
and support ever since. Before I had my PHB,

01:01:57.240 --> 01:02:02.150
my care was in the hands of an agency who
provided PAs who would come and live with

01:02:02.150 --> 01:02:05.980
me for anywhere between 24 hours and three
weeks at a time. I had no say in who the agency

01:02:05.980 --> 01:02:09.970
would send to me, and they had a very high
turnover of staff, which meant that I had

01:02:09.970 --> 01:02:15.360
no continuity of care and a constant procession
of strangers through my front door. I lived

01:02:15.360 --> 01:02:19.380
at the mercy of whoever the agency sent to
me, and because they were often short-staffed,

01:02:19.380 --> 01:02:24.190
I often had PAs who were completely unsuitable.
I never knew whether I would have a PA in

01:02:24.190 --> 01:02:28.110
place who would be capable of getting me out
of bed safely, never mind anything else that

01:02:28.110 --> 01:02:32.170
I might have wanted to do with my day, so
I wasn’t able to make commitments.

01:02:32.170 --> 01:02:36.690
My PHB has enabled me to take control of my
care, and it has given me my life back. I

01:02:36.690 --> 01:02:42.380
use my PHB to employ my own PAs directly.
I now employ two full-time PAs, and my current

01:02:42.380 --> 01:02:47.240
PAs have been with me for 18 months and two
years. I’ve gone from having 36 different

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:53.300
PAs in 2014, which was my last full year with
the agency, to having five PAs providing my

01:02:53.300 --> 01:02:58.680
care in 2018. Having the right support in
place that suits me and my needs, and having

01:02:58.680 --> 01:03:02.570
the choice of control in how that care and
support is provided, has meant that I’ve

01:03:02.570 --> 01:03:06.820
been able to return to work, which just wasn’t
possible before. I’m back to having an active

01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:11.741
social life, and my health has improved because
my PAs know me. They know my needs, and they

01:03:11.741 --> 01:03:14.100
are able to provide proactive, preventative
care.

01:03:14.100 --> 01:03:19.530
I now hold a role as a member of the personalised
care strategic coproduction group, which means

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:24.320
that I am able to use my experience, my knowledge
and skills to provide invaluable and experiential

01:03:24.320 --> 01:03:30.230
learning to balance the system-level priorities
with what matters most to people. We work

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:33.300
to ensure that what it offered through the
programme is clear, empowering, outcomes-focused,

01:03:33.300 --> 01:03:41.170
and that it makes sense to people. We particularly
welcome the plans to recruit 500 new peer

01:03:41.170 --> 01:03:46.090
leaders, as James was saying, to scale the
work that we’re already doing along with

01:03:46.090 --> 01:03:49.750
coproduction. As members of the strategic
coproduction group, we hold roles on the personalised

01:03:49.750 --> 01:03:54.660
care advisory board, to inform and influence
policy and strategy, and we’ve been involved

01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:59.090
in the development of universal personalised
care from the outset, ensuring that the voices

01:03:59.090 --> 01:04:03.770
and insight of people who have experienced
a personalised approach first hand are woven

01:04:03.770 --> 01:04:08.520
through the delivery plan from start to finish.
So, we’re really excited to share in today’s

01:04:08.520 --> 01:04:11.900
plans, and the commitment to see many more
people benefit from personalised care.

01:04:11.900 --> 01:04:18.740
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: Thank you, Robyn. And just
to announce today our latest figures in relation

01:04:18.740 --> 01:04:25.130
to personal health budgets, so increasing
the opportunity that Robyn’s had for many

01:04:25.130 --> 01:04:30.790
more people. We now have 40,344 people who
are receiving a personal health budget, which

01:04:30.790 --> 01:04:37.060
is our quarter-three figure, which means that
we have already achieved our target of 40,000

01:04:37.060 --> 01:04:41.760
personal health budgets that we established
for this year. It’s a big increase on the

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:48.460
last few years, over 300% increase, and we’re
seeing considerable benefits to people across

01:04:48.460 --> 01:04:53.960
the country through personal health budgets.
Just to finish off in relation to the plan,

01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:59.530
the really important thing about the plan
is that it’s got widespread support. We’ve

01:04:59.530 --> 01:05:04.200
worked with over 50 organisations who are
supporting the plans that we’ve got here

01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:09.250
today. We’ve got a significant programme
of activity, with 21 actions in the plan that

01:05:09.250 --> 01:05:15.350
we want to take forward. That includes a significant
programme of training and focus on supporting

01:05:15.350 --> 01:05:19.780
the frontline, including supporting the development
of primary care networks that we’ve just

01:05:19.780 --> 01:05:25.150
heard about at the meeting. So, really pleased
to present the paper to the Board today, and

01:05:25.150 --> 01:05:27.720
welcome any questions. Thank you, Chair.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, James,

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:32.460
and Robyn, thank you especially for coming
today. It sort of brings it alive to everybody,

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:36.970
I think, just how important this is and the
impact it can have on getting your life back

01:05:36.970 --> 01:05:41.330
together again, really.
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Absolutely, yeah.

01:05:41.330 --> 01:05:44.540
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. So, thank you, especially.
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Thank you for the opportunity

01:05:44.540 --> 01:05:45.540
to share.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Michelle?

01:05:45.540 --> 01:05:49.070
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thank you, Robyn, and
thank you, James. I think it was maybe 18

01:05:49.070 --> 01:05:53.390
months, two years ago, when you first came
to commissioning committee and then to the

01:05:53.390 --> 01:06:00.450
Board with some of your original reflections
and thinking on this area. And to many of

01:06:00.450 --> 01:06:05.160
us, it makes absolute sense, this change.
But I think one of the challenges was, how

01:06:05.160 --> 01:06:11.550
do we move it from a range of local initiatives
or initiatives across parts of the country,

01:06:11.550 --> 01:06:16.260
into an evidence-based model of change? And
I think this absolutely does that, so I want

01:06:16.260 --> 01:06:23.690
to congratulate you not only on your leadership,
but on the speed in which you have taken forward

01:06:23.690 --> 01:06:29.460
the learning from many different sectors,
built a collaboration, a coalition of support

01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:34.480
of over 50 organisations and charities and
community groups, but also clearly citizens,

01:06:34.480 --> 01:06:41.920
who this makes a huge difference to. It’s
a great, great piece of work, James, really.

01:06:41.920 --> 01:06:47.000
Couple of points and reflections from me;
if you could touch upon this, this would be

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:54.110
great. Firstly, the voluntary sector has been
very involved for 20, 30 years in what we

01:06:54.110 --> 01:07:00.790
now call social prescribing and personalised
care. How will the NHS work with the voluntary

01:07:00.790 --> 01:07:06.990
sector to ensure that we’re partners in
the delivery of this really exciting programme?

01:07:06.990 --> 01:07:14.230
And secondly, I think the personalised care
has a huge, huge opportunity to help reduce

01:07:14.230 --> 01:07:19.700
inequalities, and I’d really like you to
expand a bit further on how this programme

01:07:19.700 --> 01:07:24.250
of work can really reach people who we most
need to reach.

01:07:24.250 --> 01:07:29.980
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: Thank you very much, Michelle,
and thank you very much for your very warm

01:07:29.980 --> 01:07:34.650
comments. Of course, you know, this has been
about a huge partnership, and I’m really

01:07:34.650 --> 01:07:39.480
grateful to the support of my team, in particular,
Nicola Kay and Rich Watts, who’ve put a

01:07:39.480 --> 01:07:43.520
huge effort into creating the report, but
also the partners that we’ve worked with.

01:07:43.520 --> 01:07:49.520
There’s been input from a huge array of
people over the past couple of years.

01:07:49.520 --> 01:07:54.670
In terms of the voluntary sector, clearly,
the voluntary sector plays a really significant

01:07:54.670 --> 01:07:59.950
role in the delivery of this plan, and we
are extending our commitment in the document

01:07:59.950 --> 01:08:04.750
to work extensively with the voluntary sector
around how we take the next stage forward.

01:08:04.750 --> 01:08:11.310
The plan very much builds on the work of programmes
like Realising the Value, and the VCSE review,

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:15.300
and builds on work that the Health and Wellbeing
Alliance have done, and work of organisations

01:08:15.300 --> 01:08:20.811
such as National Voices, and the Coalition
for Collaborative Care, as well. So, our commitment

01:08:20.811 --> 01:08:24.239
to continuing to work with those organisations
to find the best way in which the voluntary

01:08:24.239 --> 01:08:30.009
sector can engage in this is incredibly important.
I think the key thing is that we now have

01:08:30.009 --> 01:08:35.390
developed an infrastructure for how this can
operate, and I mentioned before about how

01:08:35.390 --> 01:08:40.080
fragmented the system had been, and I think
a lot of the relationships and a lot of the

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:45.430
provision that was in place was fairly fragile.
What we’ll do with this structure is give

01:08:45.430 --> 01:08:50.430
local systems the permission to engage in
a different way with the voluntary sector,

01:08:50.430 --> 01:08:54.790
and we’re confident that that’s going
to produce some really interesting results,

01:08:54.790 --> 01:08:57.859
because in the areas that we’ve been piloting
this across the country, in about a third

01:08:57.859 --> 01:09:01.640
of the country now that’s engaged with this,
we’ve seen some excellent collaborations

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:06.940
between health and social care, and the voluntary
sector, to create opportunities for co-commissioning

01:09:06.940 --> 01:09:11.350
services and opportunities to work together,
and we’ve got a specific action to work

01:09:11.350 --> 01:09:16.990
with other different departments, with voluntary-sector
providers, including people like the Big Lottery

01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:23.210
Fund, and also other arm’s-length bodies,
including Public Health England, to look at

01:09:23.210 --> 01:09:27.980
ways that we can make that funding for the
voluntary sector sustainable to support this.

01:09:27.980 --> 01:09:32.239
Just in terms of health inequalities in particular,
if you take something like patient activation

01:09:32.239 --> 01:09:37.400
measurement, a recent Health Foundation report
found that those with the lowest skills, confidence

01:09:37.400 --> 01:09:43.030
and knowledge who were registering as what’s
called PAM level one, patient activation measurement

01:09:43.030 --> 01:09:50.109
level one, were likely to face the highest
level of inequality. And if you work with

01:09:50.109 --> 01:09:55.700
them to increase their skills and knowledge,
and you’re actually shifting them from level

01:09:55.700 --> 01:09:59.020
one to level four, with the highest level
of skills, knowledge and confidence, you could

01:09:59.020 --> 01:10:07.060
reduce GP attendance by 19% and hospital admissions
by 38%. So, I think there’s some really

01:10:07.060 --> 01:10:12.460
strong evidence now, as you said, that working
with people in this personalised way can actively

01:10:12.460 --> 01:10:17.310
tap into the assets that they bring to their
own care, looking at building the support

01:10:17.310 --> 01:10:23.170
that exists within the community around them,
and actually enabling them to have the choice

01:10:23.170 --> 01:10:27.110
and control that they want over care, that
they perhaps do not have at the moment.

01:10:27.110 --> 01:10:33.630
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Okay. Thank you, James.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I’d just reiterate how

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:37.950
– that this is all excellent. It’s very
comprehensive, it’s very clear. But I’ve

01:10:37.950 --> 01:10:45.690
got a sort of general question on this, which
is, personalised care is not just equivalent

01:10:45.690 --> 01:10:54.130
to personal budgets. And I guess the question,
for the three of you, is, Robyn got real benefit

01:10:54.130 --> 01:10:59.940
from having a personal health budget. How
do we ensure that the people that don’t

01:10:59.940 --> 01:11:07.240
want to have personal health budgets get exactly
what Robyn can get without that financial

01:11:07.240 --> 01:11:11.090
element? And that’s the bit I still sort
of struggle with a bit, because I listen to

01:11:11.090 --> 01:11:14.620
you, Robyn, I sort of sit there and think,
‘Actually, you should have been able to

01:11:14.620 --> 01:11:18.900
get that stuff without having a personal health
budget’, so what is there in the way we

01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:24.020
do this that helps deliver that for people
who don’t want the personal health budgets?

01:11:24.020 --> 01:11:29.870
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, just in terms of the programme,
as you quite rightly say, Richard, the personal

01:11:29.870 --> 01:11:32.540
health budgets are not going to work for everybody.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: No.

01:11:32.540 --> 01:11:37.090
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: And within the figures that we’re
setting out, our intention to support 2.5

01:11:37.090 --> 01:11:41.580
million people over the next five years, we
anticipate that 200,000 people will have a

01:11:41.580 --> 01:11:46.210
personal health budget, so it shows that the
significant majority of people will experience

01:11:46.210 --> 01:11:49.940
other parts of the model, whether that’s
social prescribing, support through shared

01:11:49.940 --> 01:11:54.390
decision-making, having a much more active
care plan. And actually using the model to

01:11:54.390 --> 01:11:59.520
engage with people opens up those opportunities
to develop packages of support in a way that

01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:03.350
matters to them, without the need for a personal
health budget. But in certain areas of the

01:12:03.350 --> 01:12:08.550
system, we’re finding huge opportunity to
give people that additional control. So, if

01:12:08.550 --> 01:12:13.739
you look at something like wheelchair services,
for example, an example recently of a young

01:12:13.739 --> 01:12:21.330
man called Dylan who wanted a different wheelchair
that was higher than the cost of the wheelchairs

01:12:21.330 --> 01:12:25.450
that were being provided in the area. He’s
a gentleman with cerebral palsy, needed electronic

01:12:25.450 --> 01:12:31.121
foot plates in order to be able to get in
and out of his wheelchair on his own. And

01:12:31.121 --> 01:12:38.421
whilst he could access certain features within
the system, having a personal health budget

01:12:38.421 --> 01:12:43.240
enabled him to combine money from the NHS
with money from adult social care, and through

01:12:43.240 --> 01:12:48.380
that integrated budget, he was able to buy
a higher-specification wheelchair that has

01:12:48.380 --> 01:12:53.890
given him the independence to go to university
without the need of a carer. And it’s actually

01:12:53.890 --> 01:12:58.230
cost, you know, I think £2,000 extra for
his wheelchair, and at the same time, saved

01:12:58.230 --> 01:13:03.500
£13,000 in care costs. So, trying to get
that shift where it matters is important,

01:13:03.500 --> 01:13:07.420
but I take your point. I mean, it’s not
going to be the right model for everybody.

01:13:07.420 --> 01:13:14.360
I don’t know, Robyn, if you want to comment?
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Yeah. So, I think in regards

01:13:14.360 --> 01:13:18.880
to personal health budgets, not everybody
will want to have a direct payment option,

01:13:18.880 --> 01:13:23.610
so there’s three different ways of managing
them. So, you can have a notional budget,

01:13:23.610 --> 01:13:28.190
a third-party budget or a direct payment option,
so that if you don’t want to have the financial

01:13:28.190 --> 01:13:32.871
responsibility of having a direct payment,
as I do, then there’s that option. But as

01:13:32.871 --> 01:13:38.421
James was saying, the whole context of personalised
care is about opening up a different conversation

01:13:38.421 --> 01:13:43.210
and being able to work in partnership with
health professionals, so that instead of being

01:13:43.210 --> 01:13:48.270
dictated to and being told how your health
condition can rule your life, you can work

01:13:48.270 --> 01:13:52.790
with your health professionals to work around
your health condition and really get to grips

01:13:52.790 --> 01:13:55.190
with what matters to people.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Thank you.

01:13:55.190 --> 01:13:59.590
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: So, thank you James and Robyn,
and thank you, James, again for all the hard

01:13:59.590 --> 01:14:06.250
work. It’s fantastic. And thank you, Robyn,
for so wonderfully describing what it actually

01:14:06.250 --> 01:14:11.190
means to you as an individual. And actually,
I just wanted to follow on from the comment

01:14:11.190 --> 01:14:15.360
you just made about the professionals who
you work with. And I think James touched on

01:14:15.360 --> 01:14:20.340
this a little bit. So, as this programme expands,
it will mean changes for the way our frontline

01:14:20.340 --> 01:14:26.400
staff work. And I just wondered if you could
expand a little bit and give us a sense of

01:14:26.400 --> 01:14:30.950
what those changes will be and how we support
our staff making the changes.

01:14:30.950 --> 01:14:36.420
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, the really positive thing
is that we have thousands of staff across

01:14:36.420 --> 01:14:43.940
the country that are already delivering excellent
personalised care in terms of, you know, our

01:14:43.940 --> 01:14:48.190
GP profession and the many nurses and other
healthcare professionals that we have in the

01:14:48.190 --> 01:14:53.400
system. We recognise though that training’s
going to be a significant requirement of this,

01:14:53.400 --> 01:14:58.400
and we’re committing to train at least 75,000
people over the next few years, to equip them

01:14:58.400 --> 01:15:03.460
with the skills of how to have that different
conversation, how to have the shared decision-making

01:15:03.460 --> 01:15:08.630
conversation, in 30 clinical pathways, for
example. We’re working on specific tools

01:15:08.630 --> 01:15:13.190
across lots of clinical areas. We’re also
working with the Royal College of GPs, the

01:15:13.190 --> 01:15:18.250
Royal College of Nurses, the Royal College
of OTs. We intend to work with them to look

01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:23.820
at how we can embed personalised care training
within curriculums right at the beginning,

01:15:23.820 --> 01:15:28.170
and we’ve got some fantastic support from
colleagues across the system in order to do

01:15:28.170 --> 01:15:33.200
that. But we recognise there’s also a cultural
shift that needs to happen here, as well,

01:15:33.200 --> 01:15:38.000
so continuing to work with our partners in
local communities. And I think the really

01:15:38.000 --> 01:15:42.570
important role that Robyn and her colleagues
as peer leaders play in actually talking to

01:15:42.570 --> 01:15:46.090
people about the difference this has made
to their lives, and starting to shift that

01:15:46.090 --> 01:15:51.510
focus of how things can be different if we
just change the way in which we approach things.

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:55.920
But the package of support that we’re putting
in place within universal personalised care

01:15:55.920 --> 01:15:58.595
really concentrates on equipping the workforce
with the skills that they need to deliver

01:15:58.595 --> 01:15:59.595
this.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ruth?

01:15:59.595 --> 01:16:05.670
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Just very quickly, it’s excellent
and fantastic, and really inspirational, Robyn

01:16:05.670 --> 01:16:14.050
in particular, just to see how it’s had
great impact on your health. But as well,

01:16:14.050 --> 01:16:18.970
it’s opened doors, again, for your social
life, and indeed getting back into work. And

01:16:18.970 --> 01:16:23.590
I guess that’s what struck me in your example
as well as Robyn’s example, is how this

01:16:23.590 --> 01:16:29.420
is really tackling health inequalities. It’s
putting policy into actual practice, so that’s

01:16:29.420 --> 01:16:32.400
just fantastic.
Your last point, then, was about how you’re

01:16:32.400 --> 01:16:36.880
getting the training for, is it 60,000 people?
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: 75,000, we’re aiming

01:16:36.880 --> 01:16:39.930
for.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: 75,000, okay. Even better. Fantastic.

01:16:39.930 --> 01:16:46.500
I’d like to offer my personal support in
coming into this new role about how we support

01:16:46.500 --> 01:16:50.980
the whole care profession, not just nurses
in this, but the whole care profession, about

01:16:50.980 --> 01:16:54.980
how we can do that. Very happy to work with
you in that, and well done, again. It’s

01:16:54.980 --> 01:16:58.020
been fantastic to listen to you all.
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: Thank you. Thank you, Ruth.

01:16:58.020 --> 01:17:02.370
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, James and Robyn, and
Ian, thank you all of you very much. It’s

01:17:02.370 --> 01:17:03.853
fantastic.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, Chair.

01:17:03.853 --> 01:17:05.060
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: It’s just another – you

01:17:05.060 --> 01:17:10.290
know, it just fits so well with the whole
GP – work of GPs, as well. It’s just part

01:17:10.290 --> 01:17:15.190
of a package isn’t it, so it’s fantastic,
and it’s going to change the lives of tens

01:17:15.190 --> 01:17:18.830
of thousands of people. So, thank you. Hundreds
of thousands of people, actually. Yeah. So,

01:17:18.830 --> 01:17:21.660
thank you very much. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Robyn Chappell: Thank you very much.

01:17:21.660 --> 01:17:31.290
&gt;&gt;James Sanderson: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right. We’ll go on to operational

01:17:31.290 --> 01:17:37.000
performance. Matthew, do you want to kick
off on our – on performance, and bring in

01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:39.660
Pauline when you get to the winter?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Well, I think we’ll

01:17:39.660 --> 01:17:44.950
probably start with the A&amp;E and winter pieces,
Pauline, and then you hand back to me, if

01:17:44.950 --> 01:17:47.170
that’s all right?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Okay, so I’m going to

01:17:47.170 --> 01:17:53.440
report on urgent and emergency care, and then
say a little bit about elective. In reporting

01:17:53.440 --> 01:17:59.430
back on urgent and emergency care, I think
it’s probably important to remind the Board

01:17:59.430 --> 01:18:04.540
of the conversations that we’ve been having
over the course of the last 18 months or so

01:18:04.540 --> 01:18:13.350
about our reform agenda, the agenda that very
much highlights the fact that we are leading

01:18:13.350 --> 01:18:20.270
a major transformation project which will
mean for our population that when they need

01:18:20.270 --> 01:18:25.480
urgent and emergency care or when they perceive
they need urgent and emergency care, that

01:18:25.480 --> 01:18:31.040
instead of attending the A&amp;E department in
the same way that somebody would have done

01:18:31.040 --> 01:18:37.850
in 1948, that instead they pick up the phone,
they dial 111 and then we are able to direct

01:18:37.850 --> 01:18:44.190
you to the service that best meets your needs.
I wanted to mention that, because I think

01:18:44.190 --> 01:18:53.590
when we look at our performance for December
and year to date, it further gives us evidence

01:18:53.590 --> 01:18:59.210
that that reform agenda is successful and
that it was the right thing to do. So, when

01:18:59.210 --> 01:19:05.340
we look at attendances, yes, we have seen
a growth in attendances year to date, but

01:19:05.340 --> 01:19:10.480
when we look at the type of attendances, they
are to type-three departments and type-three

01:19:10.480 --> 01:19:17.720
services, so away from the core emergency
department. Likewise, when we look at admissions,

01:19:17.720 --> 01:19:24.570
we’ve seen some increase in admissions,
but mainly an increase in same-day emergency

01:19:24.570 --> 01:19:31.940
care, which again, is a very different way
of meeting the urgent or emergency care need

01:19:31.940 --> 01:19:37.830
that the patient perceives that they have.
And we’ve seen that performance continue

01:19:37.830 --> 01:19:44.840
during the month of December. Our headline
performance from a metric point of view was

01:19:44.840 --> 01:19:53.070
better than the previous December, up by about
1.2%, but I think most importantly, to recognise

01:19:53.070 --> 01:20:00.400
the fact that within four hours, we saw 3.9%
more people than we had the previous December,

01:20:00.400 --> 01:20:06.370
so the year-on-year performance. So, thanks
to the amazing work of our staff throughout

01:20:06.370 --> 01:20:12.970
the urgent and emergency care pathway, from
out of hospital, in hospital, etc., more people

01:20:12.970 --> 01:20:20.090
are being seen in a timely manner.
And that also follows through for our ambulance

01:20:20.090 --> 01:20:27.100
service. Whilst activity was slightly increased
during December, our ambulance staff working

01:20:27.100 --> 01:20:34.151
very closely with our hospital staff have
managed to significantly reduce the number

01:20:34.151 --> 01:20:40.220
of handover delays. For 30 minutes, that are
30-minute metric, we’ve seen a decrease

01:20:40.220 --> 01:20:47.030
of 25%; and for the 60-minute metric, a decrease
of 40%, which I think is quite phenomenal,

01:20:47.030 --> 01:20:53.610
and again, is testament to the work that people
are doing in hospital and in the ambulance

01:20:53.610 --> 01:20:58.410
service, but I would stress most of all the
fact that they are working together on a day-to-day

01:20:58.410 --> 01:21:03.210
basis to deliver this.
On our overall ambulance standards, which

01:21:03.210 --> 01:21:10.239
we introduced last year as part of the reform
agenda, again, we’ve seen an ongoing improvement

01:21:10.239 --> 01:21:17.590
in performance for all of those standards.
For our cat-one performance, we record in

01:21:17.590 --> 01:21:24.450
the paper a performance of seven minutes six
seconds for response time, and that was an

01:21:24.450 --> 01:21:31.140
increase of about one minute – sorry, an
improvement of about one minute 46 seconds

01:21:31.140 --> 01:21:39.400
on the previous winter.
When we look at our 111 service, our year-to-date

01:21:39.400 --> 01:21:48.850
activity is significantly increased, about
800,000 more calls. And really importantly,

01:21:48.850 --> 01:21:57.390
our call answering times for December, our
60-second metric up by about 10% year on year,

01:21:57.390 --> 01:22:03.570
and most importantly, the number of people
receiving clinical advice through 111 up to

01:22:03.570 --> 01:22:15.370
53% from 46% last year. Also, 111 online being
rolled out across England and by December

01:22:15.370 --> 01:22:26.830
reaching about 91.5% of areas.
When we look at our overall work on our discharge

01:22:26.830 --> 01:22:33.420
and length of stay, again, we see an improvement.
If we look at the patients who’ve been with

01:22:33.420 --> 01:22:42.190
us for – in our acute hospitals for more
than 21 days, we see a decrease of 1,739 from

01:22:42.190 --> 01:22:49.660
when the baseline was established last spring.
An ongoing improvement in DTOC. And I think

01:22:49.660 --> 01:22:57.190
some of that is definitely contributed to
the fact – sorry, influenced by the fact

01:22:57.190 --> 01:23:04.550
that we had the allocation of social care
monies, the £240 million, and I think all

01:23:04.550 --> 01:23:10.969
reports back from throughout the Service is
that we’ve had very good system-working

01:23:10.969 --> 01:23:18.290
between our Health Service staff, our local
authority staff, our social care staff, etc.,

01:23:18.290 --> 01:23:22.930
in preparation for this winter, but in delivery
of care.

01:23:22.930 --> 01:23:28.250
Our overall winter arrangements have been
put in place in a similar fashion to last

01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:34.950
year, and that’s important because throughout
the winter period, we clearly do face challenges.

01:23:34.950 --> 01:23:42.050
We have a small number of organisations that
we are providing intensive support to, and

01:23:42.050 --> 01:23:49.650
clearly, we have got challenging times ahead
of us, as well as over the last couple of

01:23:49.650 --> 01:23:58.640
weeks, around flu and with respect to weather.
Just to say a little bit more on flu. The

01:23:58.640 --> 01:24:04.720
first comment is that the vaccine that we’re
using, we’ve seen a very good match with

01:24:04.720 --> 01:24:13.960
the virus that – the dominant virus, which
has been H1N1, and that has meant that as

01:24:13.960 --> 01:24:22.290
far as our ability as a Service to manage
flu, the issues have been predominantly around

01:24:22.290 --> 01:24:30.660
HDU/ITU, H1N1. It usually impacts adult patients,
and when they’re poorly, they tend to need

01:24:30.660 --> 01:24:38.500
high-dependency care. So, when we look across
our hospital sector, our HDU and ITU beds

01:24:38.500 --> 01:24:47.540
during December were at about the same level
from a flu perspective as the previous December,

01:24:47.540 --> 01:24:57.010
but in our general adult beds, we saw very
few patients. From a vaccination perspective,

01:24:57.010 --> 01:25:03.080
the numbers are broadly in line with last
year for the public, and for our staff vaccination

01:25:03.080 --> 01:25:08.960
programme, we’ve seen a higher response
than the previous year.

01:25:08.960 --> 01:25:15.500
So, that’s my update really on urgent and
emergency care. I’ll just move very quickly

01:25:15.500 --> 01:25:23.390
to RTT. And again, I think it’s worthwhile
setting it in the context of an overall reform

01:25:23.390 --> 01:25:33.310
programme. We have seen on our headline metrics
our waiting list continuing to decrease. We

01:25:33.310 --> 01:25:39.300
have seen real progress as far as long waits
are concerned; that’s patients who are waiting

01:25:39.300 --> 01:25:46.020
more than 52 weeks. If we look at performance
over a five-month period, the numbers were

01:25:46.020 --> 01:25:55.690
down by just over 1,000. At the same time,
CCGs are managing demand in line with the

01:25:55.690 --> 01:26:03.180
priorities that we set in the reform programme,
and if we look at GP referrals over a period

01:26:03.180 --> 01:26:12.600
of, say, ‘15/16 to ‘17/18, we see that
significant drop from 4.7% down to 1.8% of

01:26:12.600 --> 01:26:19.770
growth in referrals, and really, at the moment,
we are just about flatlined. And at the same

01:26:19.770 --> 01:26:26.750
time, a lot of work taking place in the development
of alternative services to support that, for

01:26:26.750 --> 01:26:34.190
example, our MSK triage services, and overall
work across 12 transformation areas, and the

01:26:34.190 --> 01:26:40.580
production of handbooks that support the Service.
So, that’s my high-level report.

01:26:40.580 --> 01:26:45.050
&gt;&gt;David Prior. Thank you very much. Matthew,
do you want to finish off on observations

01:26:45.050 --> 01:26:46.050
and –
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yeah, thank you. Thank

01:26:46.050 --> 01:26:49.520
you, Pauline. That’s brilliant, and I think
that that theme of the changes that we’re

01:26:49.520 --> 01:26:52.720
bringing in in terms of the way the service
is delivered both across emergency and elective

01:26:52.720 --> 01:26:57.120
care dovetails very neatly into the conversation
we had about the new GP contract and where

01:26:57.120 --> 01:27:05.080
Ian was taking us, this coming together, and
the musculoskeletal triage service covering

01:27:05.080 --> 01:27:11.760
98% of the country now, meaning that people
can contact that and be referred directly

01:27:11.760 --> 01:27:15.870
to a physiotherapist rather than being referred
into an orthopaedics outpatient. That’s

01:27:15.870 --> 01:27:23.300
happened to me, so it’s a real service.
The – in other areas, I probably should

01:27:23.300 --> 01:27:30.620
follow through that, just talk a bit about
where we are with digital services. The NHS

01:27:30.620 --> 01:27:36.350
website which we’ve talked before about
having moved to – upgraded for NHS UK, in

01:27:36.350 --> 01:27:43.960
December 2018, it saw 10% more – 4 million
more visits than it had seen in December the

01:27:43.960 --> 01:27:48.330
year before, and December the year before
had been when we were just running into the

01:27:48.330 --> 01:27:53.450
flu outbreak and it had been very high numbers
then. We’d seen the highest ever numbers

01:27:53.450 --> 01:28:00.640
then, as well, so we see a continued growth.
So, 40 million visits a month now on NHS website.

01:28:00.640 --> 01:28:08.390
And we’ve now got 80 apps live on the app
library, with another 117 being reviewed for

01:28:08.390 --> 01:28:12.550
launch into the app library, so that’s – I
think that’s been… But the – I think

01:28:12.550 --> 01:28:19.890
the big news for us was, 31st December, we
launched the NHS app, and we are in the process

01:28:19.890 --> 01:28:25.580
now of working through with GP practices,
connecting the app to every practice, because

01:28:25.580 --> 01:28:31.230
the – at the moment, anybody I think can
download through their – through the Apple

01:28:31.230 --> 01:28:35.980
or Google websites. The app – if your GP
isn’t yet connected, you’ll have access

01:28:35.980 --> 01:28:42.469
to nhs.uk information, and you’ll have access
to 111 through it. As your GPs are connected,

01:28:42.469 --> 01:28:47.950
you’ll also have access to booking and accessing
your GP record, and so on. So, I think that’s

01:28:47.950 --> 01:28:53.120
big progress, and over the next year, I would
expect that to roll out to offer the full

01:28:53.120 --> 01:29:01.830
services to all patients across the country.
In other areas, to talk about cancer, because

01:29:01.830 --> 01:29:09.540
we’ve discussed that a lot at the Board.
We’re seeing some improvement in our cancer

01:29:09.540 --> 01:29:14.969
performance, but we are still missing the
national cancer standard for 62-day performance.

01:29:14.969 --> 01:29:18.960
We’re now achieving it – we’re back
achieving it in breast and skin cancers, but

01:29:18.960 --> 01:29:23.610
we have other cancer types where we’re not
making it, although, I say, we’re seeing

01:29:23.610 --> 01:29:29.490
some improvement. We’ve seen in the past
year a 10% increase in the number of patients

01:29:29.490 --> 01:29:36.360
being treated in the 62-day cancer pathway,
so what we’re seeing is, appropriately,

01:29:36.360 --> 01:29:42.570
a significant increase in the number of potential
cancers referred in to the NHS, a significant

01:29:42.570 --> 01:29:47.140
increase in the number of diagnoses that we’re
producing, a significant increase in the number

01:29:47.140 --> 01:29:50.610
of people who are being reassured they don’t
have cancer, and a significant increase in

01:29:50.610 --> 01:29:57.469
the number of people who are identifying with
cancer that we can start care for more quickly.

01:29:57.469 --> 01:30:05.450
We are at the moment across the country gearing
up our services to try and respond to that

01:30:05.450 --> 01:30:08.530
increase in demand, and so it’s very much
going on at the moment, and part of the planning

01:30:08.530 --> 01:30:14.340
for next year. So, we’re not complacent
about that at the moment, but as part of the

01:30:14.340 --> 01:30:20.239
Long-Term Plan strategy is to identify more
cancers earlier, this is something that needs

01:30:20.239 --> 01:30:24.180
to be a key part of our strategic direction,
which is, we need to build up our diagnostic

01:30:24.180 --> 01:30:30.570
and treatment capacity in cancer.
In primary care, we’ve now got 100% coverage

01:30:30.570 --> 01:30:39.550
of extended access across the country. Whilst
this plays very much into the conversations

01:30:39.550 --> 01:30:49.720
and the agreement that Ian’s team have now
settled of driving forward the workforce across

01:30:49.720 --> 01:30:58.820
primary care, we’ve exceeded the wider workforce
target with 5,321 additional staff recruited

01:30:58.820 --> 01:31:04.890
to date, included pharmacists and paramedics,
we are still missing the numbers of additional

01:31:04.890 --> 01:31:11.850
GPs, and we are working very hard to try and
make those numbers. We’ve got 3,473 more

01:31:11.850 --> 01:31:16.800
doctors accepted onto specialty training,
which is higher than the Health Education

01:31:16.800 --> 01:31:22.220
England target was, and 10% more than last
year, so there is some good news there.

01:31:22.220 --> 01:31:29.219
In mental health, we are – now for the first
time have 100% of CCGs on track to meet the

01:31:29.219 --> 01:31:37.350
mental health investment standard. 4,450 additional
women accessed perinatal mental health services

01:31:37.350 --> 01:31:43.550
during the first half of this financial year
than the previous year, and we’re on track

01:31:43.550 --> 01:31:48.700
to hit the access standards for children and
young people’s mental health access as well.

01:31:48.700 --> 01:31:55.370
So, I think we’re seeing progress across
the board, but still a lot of work to do in

01:31:55.370 --> 01:32:02.790
order to make sure that we land the Five Year
Forward View in advance of – as we run into

01:32:02.790 --> 01:32:07.500
the Long-Term Plan. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Matthew, thank you,

01:32:07.500 --> 01:32:12.790
Pauline. Questions for Pauline? Yeah, Joanne?
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thanks very much for that.

01:32:12.790 --> 01:32:17.560
I just wanted to pick up, Pauline, your sort
of theme about creating a better range of

01:32:17.560 --> 01:32:21.701
choices for people if they have an urgent
care need, and how that’s coming on. And

01:32:21.701 --> 01:32:26.900
I wondered if you could tell us a little bit
more about the impact that NHS 111 online

01:32:26.900 --> 01:32:31.380
is starting to have. I noticed you had a figure
of 100,000 in the paper, which seemed like

01:32:31.380 --> 01:32:37.230
a nice big number. How are patients using
it, and do you have a feel yet for what contribution

01:32:37.230 --> 01:32:41.230
it’s making or could make to the pressures
in the future?

01:32:41.230 --> 01:32:46.260
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Okay, so I think the thing
to say at this point is that a lot of the

01:32:46.260 --> 01:32:52.060
feedback we’re getting is anecdotal, but
we are searching for that feedback, and I

01:32:52.060 --> 01:32:58.820
think as with how society has changed, some
of the feedback has been very positive, because

01:32:58.820 --> 01:33:10.170
people have felt that asking them to pick
up a phone and contact 111 at a time of need,

01:33:10.170 --> 01:33:15.520
in a way that they would not do the rest of
their business, was somewhat out of date,

01:33:15.520 --> 01:33:22.710
and that they felt that being able to do it
online was maybe slightly less threatening,

01:33:22.710 --> 01:33:28.020
but they also felt that the response time
would be even quicker than it would be by

01:33:28.020 --> 01:33:32.620
phone. Now, whether that’s a reality or
not from – because we are driving up the

01:33:32.620 --> 01:33:36.690
response times as far as our call answering
is concerned, but I think it’s the public

01:33:36.690 --> 01:33:43.290
perception, and that in all walks of life,
we are behaving differently. So, all of the

01:33:43.290 --> 01:33:47.600
feedback that we have received to date, anecdotal
as it is, is very positive.

01:33:47.600 --> 01:33:53.690
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Are you going to do the finance,
Matthew, or should we go to this, matters

01:33:53.690 --> 01:33:58.260
on the operational update, this – do you
just want to update the Board where we are

01:33:58.260 --> 01:34:05.860
on finance, as well?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Style: Okay, so briefly, yes, it’s

01:34:05.860 --> 01:34:12.680
at the back of the paper. So, the numbers
in the paper reflect the forecast at month

01:34:12.680 --> 01:34:20.580
eight. The forecast currently has us overdelivering
on our planned underspend of £265 million

01:34:20.580 --> 01:34:23.070
for the full year by a further £188 million,
taking us to £453 million planned underspend

01:34:23.070 --> 01:34:26.760
for the year as a whole. As has been familiar
from previous years, within that, the pattern

01:34:26.760 --> 01:34:31.830
is of pressures emerging in some of the CCG
forecasts, particularly around overperformance

01:34:31.830 --> 01:34:41.020
on some contracts, offset by carefully managed
underspend in some central budgets and underperformance

01:34:41.020 --> 01:34:46.090
against the quality premium. We’ll continue
to keep the forecast under review, in particular,

01:34:46.090 --> 01:34:50.810
through a series of deep dives off the back
of the month-nine forecast, to ensure that

01:34:50.810 --> 01:34:54.900
the commissioning system can play its appropriate
part in delivering financial balance across

01:34:54.900 --> 01:34:57.760
the NHS as a whole.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any – Matthew?

01:34:57.760 --> 01:35:02.440
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I should – before we
move on, Chair, I wonder if you’ll indulge

01:35:02.440 --> 01:35:05.300
me just to speak for – talk for a moment
about EU exit –

01:35:05.300 --> 01:35:06.860
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Oh, Brexit?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes.

01:35:06.860 --> 01:35:10.760
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, just – any – just
– absolutely, yeah. Any – just any questions

01:35:10.760 --> 01:35:14.219
on the performance anyone wants to raise,
or on the money, before we move on to Brexit?

01:35:14.219 --> 01:35:18.380
No? Okay, over to you, Matthew, on Brexit.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you. Yeah, I mean,

01:35:18.380 --> 01:35:21.820
obviously, our performance reports look backwards
over our shoulder. We also have to be looking

01:35:21.820 --> 01:35:27.710
forwards to what may or may not happen at
the end of March, and I think, probably just

01:35:27.710 --> 01:35:34.860
worth saying that there’s a huge amount
of work going on across the NHS at the moment

01:35:34.860 --> 01:35:43.510
to prepare for the potential of us exiting
the European Union without a deal at the end

01:35:43.510 --> 01:35:50.570
of March. So, my team – we have set up a
central team. We have created a series of

01:35:50.570 --> 01:35:54.760
regional teams, as Simon mentioned at the
beginning. We now have over 100 – we have

01:35:54.760 --> 01:36:02.890
nearly 200 people now embedded in regional
and national teams, to coordinate and prepare

01:36:02.890 --> 01:36:11.940
for, if that is our eventuality. We’re working
with the Department of Health and Social Care

01:36:11.940 --> 01:36:19.110
who are leading on the critical factor for
us, which is the continued supply of medicines,

01:36:19.110 --> 01:36:25.300
medical equipment and non-medical consumable
goods, and the Department of Health and Social

01:36:25.300 --> 01:36:32.050
Care are working with the Department for Transport
to ensure that there are appropriate sea routes

01:36:32.050 --> 01:36:39.510
to bring in the very large volumes of – that
the NHS requires to function normally. And

01:36:39.510 --> 01:36:46.660
we are putting in place, if we – if they
do what they need to do, then the NHS is geared

01:36:46.660 --> 01:36:53.380
up to handle the flows that happen – that
will happen out of that. We are – we’ve

01:36:53.380 --> 01:36:57.350
been doing a lot of communication with the
NHS, and over the next three weeks, we will

01:36:57.350 --> 01:37:08.270
be conducting a rolling tour around the country
talking to the NHS providers and also the

01:37:08.270 --> 01:37:12.060
professional bodies, to make sure that they
know what we know, and that all of our plans

01:37:12.060 --> 01:37:19.650
are in place, and that everybody is geared
up to deal with the potential impact of those

01:37:19.650 --> 01:37:25.199
changes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any questions on Brexit from

01:37:25.199 --> 01:37:30.730
anybody? I think we’ve had a pretty thorough
discussion of this anyway, haven’t we, so

01:37:30.730 --> 01:37:38.140
it’s – no? Moving on to the sort of governance
part of the agenda, we’ve got four reports

01:37:38.140 --> 01:37:42.020
from Board committees that I think are really
for noting, unless anyone wants to raise anything?

01:37:42.020 --> 01:37:44.760
&gt;&gt;Lesley Tillotson: There is one we need to
ratify a decision on, please.

01:37:44.760 --> 01:37:48.430
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: I just – perhaps on the
Commissioning Committee, I could just formally

01:37:48.430 --> 01:37:55.050
note for the Board on both meetings. One is,
application of directions from the 28th November

01:37:55.050 --> 01:38:04.980
meeting on NHS Crawley, NHS Horsham and Mid-Sussex
CCG, and NHS East Surrey CCG, which the committee

01:38:04.980 --> 01:38:09.980
noted and agreed in that meeting.
And then we met again on 12th December, and

01:38:09.980 --> 01:38:16.280
in that meeting, there – we reviewed the
detail of a number of delegations of responsibilities,

01:38:16.280 --> 01:38:24.920
and agreed them for the following CCGs: NHS
Surrey Downs, Morecambe Bay, Southport and

01:38:24.920 --> 01:38:31.610
Formby, South Sefton, Bedfordshire, North
Lincolnshire, East Surrey, Somerset and Devon,

01:38:31.610 --> 01:38:36.940
as well as, we approved the merger of the
South Devon and Torbay CCG and the North East

01:38:36.940 --> 01:38:41.380
and West Devon CCG.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Do – we need to

01:38:41.380 --> 01:38:44.850
ratify that paper, don’t we?
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Yeah.

01:38:44.850 --> 01:38:49.170
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Can the Board ratify
that paper? Yeah? Okay, thank you. There’s

01:38:49.170 --> 01:38:54.050
a note on the litigation update, and Katherine
Ibbotson is here if we’ve got any detailed

01:38:54.050 --> 01:38:59.130
questions on that, but I think it’s just
for nothing, I think, isn’t it? Yeah? So,

01:38:59.130 --> 01:39:02.280
Katherine, I hope you didn’t come all the
way just to hear that.

01:39:02.280 --> 01:39:06.420
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: As you know, lawyers are
paid by the hour, except for our in-house

01:39:06.420 --> 01:39:13.540
legal team, who unfortunately, we don’t
have – we have huge confidence in their

01:39:13.540 --> 01:39:15.260
success, as represented in this paper. But
yeah, the clock is not running.

01:39:15.260 --> 01:39:16.290
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And is there any other business?
No? Good. Thank you very much.

01:39:16.290 --> 01:39:20.080
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Could I say something? I’m sorry,
I know the members of the public are not supposed

01:39:20.080 --> 01:39:24.710
to speak. I’ve been a nurse for 40 years
on the frontline, and I’m 70 years old,

01:39:24.710 --> 01:39:33.670
and I’m still working part time. And I’ve
never seen the NHS in such a bad state as

01:39:33.670 --> 01:39:48.570
it is now. And it upsets me a lot, because
I see the Ten-Year Plan, and all the plans,

01:39:48.570 --> 01:39:55.469
not 
the implementation of the plans, the plans.

01:39:55.469 --> 01:39:59.180
And you’ve only talked about the good aspects
of them. It’s been very self-congratulatory.

01:39:59.180 --> 01:40:01.660
There are lots of things to criticise about
it, and I’m not going to go into that now,

01:40:01.660 --> 01:40:09.680
but the one thing I do want to say, which
I have not heard in this meeting, is any real

01:40:09.680 --> 01:40:15.220
concern about the workforce that is going
to do all this wonderful work. We haven’t

01:40:15.220 --> 01:40:19.030
got enough staff now. We haven’t had enough
budget since 2010 to train staff. We haven’t

01:40:19.030 --> 01:40:22.449
got any workforce strategy. An interim one
coming in for spring, and a further one in

01:40:22.449 --> 01:40:29.199
autumn. We need something now. We need people
now, and it’s going to take years before

01:40:29.199 --> 01:40:41.680
those people are in post. And I’m sorry,
I just feel I have to say that, because I

01:40:41.680 --> 01:40:45.810
feel really strongly about this.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Well, you’re quite

01:40:45.810 --> 01:40:52.060
right to feel very strongly about it. I mean,
the workforce is our most important – it

01:40:52.060 --> 01:40:56.880
is absolutely crucial to the future of delivering
the Long-Term Plan, so you’re absolutely

01:40:56.880 --> 01:40:59.220
right to raise it.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: What are you going to do about

01:40:59.220 --> 01:41:00.450
it?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well…

01:41:00.450 --> 01:41:05.360
&gt;&gt;Speaker: It’s not even a priority. Not
even till autumn before we get a proper workforce

01:41:05.360 --> 01:41:07.660
–
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I can assure you, it is an

01:41:07.660 --> 01:41:13.050
absolute priority. We absolutely recognise
that we cannot begin to deliver the Long-Term

01:41:13.050 --> 01:41:19.050
Plan without the workforce to do it. It – so
what you’re saying is absolutely true, we

01:41:19.050 --> 01:41:20.050
absolutely –
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Well, thank you for acknowledging

01:41:20.050 --> 01:41:21.050
that, but I don’t know what can be done
about it. It’s too late to do anything about

01:41:21.050 --> 01:41:24.420
it now. It’s going to take three years,
and a massive amount of work at the very minimum

01:41:24.420 --> 01:41:35.630
to get enough nurses.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah. Well, the workforce is

01:41:35.630 --> 01:41:39.620
fundamentally important, and actually, you’ve
raised it actually very powerfully. So, thank

01:41:39.620 --> 01:41:40.620
you.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Well, thank you. I just wish you

01:41:40.620 --> 01:41:48.880
could do something about it.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right. Thank you. Excuse me,

01:41:48.880 --> 01:41:55.680
don’t rush off. Why don’t you stay – just
stay and have a chat outside the meeting?

01:41:55.680 --> 01:42:00.180
Just don’t – just hang on for a minute.
Good, I think that finishes the meeting. Good,

01:42:00.180 --> 01:42:01.090
thank you.

